var e={"market:saas":{intro:`Software that companies rent by the seat, month after month: customer databases, office suites, payroll and HR, accounting and team chat. The vendor runs it on its own servers, ships the updates and keeps the data, and staff use it through a web browser or an app.`,how:`Vendors compete on features, on how well they connect to the other tools a company already uses, and on how painful they are to leave. Each extra customer costs little to serve, so most of the money goes on salespeople and engineers, and gross margins near 80% are normal. Subscriptions renew every year, so investors judge these firms on recurring revenue and on how few customers cancel.`,history:`By 2013 renting business software had gone mainstream: Salesforce's yearly revenue passed $3 billion that year. In 2013 Adobe stopped selling boxed Creative Suite and moved its customers to Creative Cloud subscriptions. In 2017 Microsoft reported that Office 365 earned more from businesses than its boxed Office licences. The pandemic of 2020 sent offices home overnight, and Teams, Zoom and Slack became their workplaces. Salesforce agreed in 2020 to buy Slack for $27.7 billion. In 2024 Salesforce launched Agentforce, AI agents meant to do the work the software used to record.`,facts:[`Salesforce's revenue reached $34.9 billion in its 2024 fiscal year.`,`Microsoft Teams had 320 million monthly users in 2023.`,`Salesforce paid $27.7 billion for Slack, closing the deal in 2021.`]},"market:dbms":{intro:`Databases are the programs where organisations keep their records: accounts, bookings, stock, customers. They store the data safely, find any record in a fraction of a second, and keep it correct while thousands of people change it at once. Moving years of data to another system is risky and slow, so customers stay for a very long time.`,how:`Buyers choose on reliability, speed and the skills their staff already have. Vendors charge a licence per processor or per user, then a yearly support fee that brings most of the profit. Every program a customer writes depends on one database's quirks, so changing means rewriting them: that lock-in is the market's wealth, and only a big shift in computing gives rivals an opening.`,history:`IBM's IMS, built with North American Rockwell for the Apollo moon programme, became a product in 1969. In 1970 IBM researcher Edgar Codd proposed the relational model: data kept in simple tables and asked for by what you want, not where it lies. Larry Ellison's young company shipped Oracle Version 2, the first commercial SQL database, in 1979. The free databases MySQL (1995) and PostgreSQL (1996) put a capable database within reach of every web startup. Google described its giant Bigtable store in 2006, starting a wave of simpler, massive NoSQL databases. Snowflake, a database run entirely in the cloud, raised $3.4 billion at its listing in 2020, then the biggest software flotation ever.`,facts:[`Codd's 1970 paper was called A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks.`,`Snowflake raised $3.4 billion when it listed in 2020.`,`Oracle bought MySQL's owner Sun Microsystems for $7.4 billion in 2010.`]},"market:consumer":{intro:`The apps people open many times a day: social networks, messengers, photo and video feeds, music and dating. Most are free and earn from advertising, with some selling subscriptions. The more of your friends already use one, the more useful it is to you, so the big ones keep getting bigger.`,how:`Apps compete for attention, the minutes people spend in them, because attention is what advertisers buy. Network effects protect the leaders, but young users drift to whatever is new, so the giants buy or copy rising rivals. The costs are servers, engineers and payments to creators or record labels; real profit arrives only with hundreds of millions of users.`,history:`Facebook listed on the stock market in 2012, weeks after buying Instagram for $1 billion. In 2014 Facebook paid $19 billion for WhatsApp, a messenger with about 450 million users. Instagram copied Snapchat's disappearing Stories in 2016. By 2017 more people used Instagram Stories each day than used all of Snapchat. TikTok said it had a billion monthly users in 2021, the same year Facebook renamed itself Meta. Threads, Meta's answer to Twitter, gained 100 million sign-ups in its first five days in 2023.`,facts:[`Facebook passed 1 billion monthly users in 2012.`,`Spotify passed 100 million paying subscribers in 2019.`,`Meta counted over 3 billion people using its apps every day in 2023.`]},"market:devtools":{intro:`The tools programmers use to write, test and ship software: compilers that turn code into machine instructions, editors, debuggers, version control that tracks every change, and build and testing systems. Developers choose their own tools and keep them for years, so a tool that wins their habits keeps them.`,how:`Compilers once came bundled with the computer; independent firms sell faster or friendlier ones by the copy or by the seat. Many tools are free and earn from companies paying for teams, support or a hosted version. Tools compete on speed, on how well they suit a language, and on what other programmers already use, since skills and files move with people.`,history:`IBM began charging separately for software in 1970, which opened the door for independent firms to sell programmers' tools. At Bell Labs in 1972 Marc Rochkind wrote SCCS, the first version-control system. Borland's Turbo Pascal of 1983 sold for $49.95 and compiled programs in seconds on a PC. In 2005 Linus Torvalds wrote Git in a few weeks to manage the Linux code. GitHub launched in 2008 as a social home for Git projects. In 2021 GitHub Copilot began suggesting code as programmers typed.`,facts:[`Turbo Pascal cost $49.95 in 1983, when rival compilers cost hundreds of dollars.`,`Microsoft paid $7.5 billion for GitHub in 2018.`,`GitHub counted over 100 million developers in 2023.`]},"market:os":{intro:`The operating system is the program that runs the computer itself: it starts the machine, manages memory and disks, and gives every other program a common way to draw on the screen, read files and print. Applications are written for one system, so people pick the system that runs the programs they need.`,how:`The system is sold with each machine, or licensed to many hardware makers for a fee per copy. Developers write for the system with the most users, and users buy the system with the most programs: this loop makes a leader almost impossible to dislodge. Rivals break in only when a new kind of computer appears, or by giving their system away.`,history:`Gary Kildall's CP/M, a disk operating system for small computers, went on sale in 1976. In 1981 Microsoft licensed MS-DOS to IBM for its PC, but kept the right to sell it to other makers. Apple's Macintosh brought a mouse-driven graphical system to a mass market in 1984. In 1991 a Finnish student, Linus Torvalds, released the Linux kernel free. Windows 95 sold 7 million copies in its first five weeks in 1995. Google's Android joined Apple's iPhone system in 2008 and moved the battle to phones.`,facts:[`Windows 95 sold 7 million copies in its first five weeks (1995).`,`Android ran on about seven in ten smartphones sold in 2012.`,`Windows ran on about nine in ten PCs in 2010.`]},"market:games":{intro:`Video games for phones, consoles and PCs, sold as one-off purchases, as subscriptions, or free with items to buy inside. A small team can make a hit, while a big studio's game can cost tens of millions and still flop. Players are children, students, commuters and grown-ups of every age.`,how:`Players buy on word of mouth, reviews and what their friends play, so launch timing and marketing matter as much as quality. Platform stores take about 30% of each sale. Free-to-play games earn from the few per cent of players who spend, while live games earn for years from updates, seasons and cosmetic items. Most releases lose money; a handful of hits pay for the rest.`,history:`In 2012 Tencent bought 40% of Epic Games for about $330 million. Grand Theft Auto V took $1 billion in its first three days in 2013. Epic made Fortnite's Battle Royale mode free in 2017, and it had 125 million players within a year. Lockdowns made 2020 a record year as people played at home. Microsoft completed its purchase of Activision Blizzard for about $69 billion in 2023. Nintendo's Switch 2 sold 3.5 million units in its first four days in 2025.`,facts:[`Grand Theft Auto V earned $1 billion in three days in 2013.`,`Roblox had over 70 million daily users in 2023.`,`Microsoft paid about $69 billion for Activision Blizzard in 2023.`]},"market:mlapi":{intro:`Machine learning sold as a service over the internet: a developer sends a photo, a sentence or a list of clicks and gets back labels, a translation, a transcript or a recommendation. The cloud company trains and runs the models, so a small app can use them without hiring a single researcher.`,how:`Customers pay per thousand requests, so the cloud with the most accurate models at the lowest price per call wins, and buyers tend to stay where their data already lives. The services often serve to bring companies onto a cloud for storage and servers. Accuracy on the customer's own pictures and words matters more than scores on public tests.`,history:`Google turned its Translate API into a paid service in 2011, after first planning to shut it. Microsoft launched Azure Machine Learning in 2014. Google released its Cloud Vision API in 2016, labelling faces, objects and text in photos. Amazon launched Rekognition, Polly and Lex together in 2016. In 2018 the American Civil Liberties Union showed Rekognition wrongly matched 28 members of Congress with arrest photos. In 2020 IBM quit general face recognition and Amazon paused police use of Rekognition.`,facts:[`The ACLU's 2018 test matched 28 members of Congress with mugshots.`,`Amazon announced three AI services, Rekognition, Polly and Lex, on one day in 2016.`]},"market:assist":{intro:`Chatbots anyone can talk to in plain language: they answer questions, write and fix text, explain homework, summarise documents and help plan a trip. Most people use them free, and a minority pay a monthly subscription for the strongest models and higher limits.`,how:`Every answer costs real computing time on expensive chips, so serving hundreds of millions of free users is a huge bill, paid by subscribers and investors. Assistants compete on how clever, fast and trustworthy their answers are, on memory and tools such as web search, and on being built into the phone, browser or office software people already use.`,history:`OpenAI released ChatGPT as a free research preview on 30 November 2022, and a million people signed up in five days. By January 2023 it had an estimated 100 million monthly users, the fastest-growing consumer app to that date. ChatGPT Plus launched in February 2023 at $20 a month. Anthropic released Claude and Google launched Bard in March 2023. Google renamed Bard as Gemini in 2024, and Meta put its assistant inside WhatsApp, Instagram and Facebook that year. In 2025 OpenAI said ChatGPT had 800 million weekly users.`,facts:[`ChatGPT reached 1 million users five days after its 2022 launch.`,`ChatGPT Plus cost $20 a month from 2023.`,`OpenAI reported 800 million weekly ChatGPT users in 2025.`]},"market:api":{intro:`Language models rented by developers over the internet and priced by the token, a chunk of text about three quarters of a word long. Startups and big companies build them into chatbots, search, support desks and their own products, rather than training a model of their own.`,how:`Customers pay separately for the text they send in and the text the model writes, usually per million tokens. Many tasks do not need the cleverest model, so cheap small models win much of the volume, and buyers switch whenever a rival gives better answers per dollar. Switching is as easy as changing one line of code, so loyalty is thin and prices fall fast.`,history:`OpenAI opened a private beta of its API, serving the GPT-3 model, in June 2020. Anthropic was founded in 2021 by former OpenAI staff. In March 2023 OpenAI cut prices tenfold with gpt-3.5-turbo, the model behind ChatGPT. OpenRouter launched in 2023 to send each request to whichever provider offered the best deal. In January 2025 China's DeepSeek released R1, a reasoning model priced at a small fraction of its rivals. Anthropic's yearly revenue rate passed $5 billion in 2025, mostly from businesses using its API.`,facts:[`gpt-3.5-turbo cost $0.002 per thousand tokens in 2023, a tenth of the older model.`,`Anthropic's annual revenue rate passed $5 billion in 2025.`]},"market:code":{intro:`AI tools that write software: autocomplete that finishes a programmer's line or a whole function, and chat that explains, writes or fixes code on request. Companies buy them per developer seat, betting that their engineers will get more done in a day.`,how:`A good assistant saves an engineer hours a week, and engineers are expensive, so companies pay readily. Tools compete on the model behind them, how well they understand a large codebase, and how smoothly they fit the editor. The more of the work a tool takes on, the more model time it uses, so the bills, and the revenue, grow with it.`,history:`GitHub previewed Copilot, built on OpenAI's Codex model, inside the Visual Studio Code editor in June 2021. Copilot opened to paying users in June 2022 at $10 a month. Cursor, an AI-first editor from the startup Anysphere, launched in 2023. In February 2025 Anthropic released Claude Code, an agent that works in the programmer's terminal. OpenAI launched its Codex agent in May 2025. In July 2025 Google paid $2.4 billion to license Windsurf's technology and hire its leaders after OpenAI's deal to buy the company fell through.`,facts:[`Copilot cost individual programmers $10 a month in 2022.`,`Cursor's yearly revenue rate passed $500 million in 2025.`,`Google paid $2.4 billion for Windsurf's leaders and a licence in 2025.`]},"market:science":{intro:`AI sold to the organisations that pay most for the cleverest models: drug makers, materials labs, banks, law firms and big companies drowning in documents. Deals are large, custom and slow to close, and often include engineers who adapt the model to the customer's own data.`,how:`Buyers pay for results they can measure: a faster search for a new material, fewer hours of lawyers' time, better fraud detection. Vendors compete on model quality, security and keeping data private, since these customers will not let secrets leave their walls. Contracts run for years and come with consultants, so trust and a strong sales force matter as much as research.`,history:`IBM put $1 billion into a new Watson business unit in 2014, promising to transform medicine. In 2017 the MD Anderson cancer centre shelved its Watson project after spending $62 million. DeepMind's AlphaFold predicted protein shapes almost as well as lab experiments at the CASP14 contest in 2020. In 2023 IBM relaunched its AI business as watsonx, and the law firm Allen & Overy rolled out Harvey, an AI assistant, to its lawyers. In 2024 Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for AlphaFold.`,facts:[`MD Anderson spent $62 million on its Watson project before halting it in 2017.`,`AlphaFold's database held over 200 million predicted protein structures in 2022.`]},"market:image":{intro:`Pictures made from a sentence: a user types what they want, and a model paints it in seconds, in any style from photograph to oil painting. Designers, marketers, game makers and hobbyists use these tools for sketches, adverts, concept art and memes.`,how:`Most image models start from random noise and remove it step by step towards a picture that matches the prompt, a method called diffusion. Users pay by subscription or per image, and each picture takes a few seconds on a graphics chip. Tools compete on beauty, on following the prompt faithfully, and on control of faces, lettering and edits.`,history:`OpenAI showed DALL-E, which drew pictures from captions, in January 2021. DALL-E 2 followed in April 2022, and Midjourney opened its beta to everyone through Discord in July 2022. Stability AI released Stable Diffusion free in August 2022, so anyone could run it on a gaming graphics card. In 2023 Getty Images sued Stability AI for copying millions of its photos. A fake Midjourney picture of the Pope in a white puffer coat fooled millions in March 2023. In August 2025 Google's Gemini image model, nicknamed Nano Banana, drew millions of new users to its app.`,facts:[`Stable Diffusion was trained in 2022 on images drawn from the 5-billion-picture LAION-5B dataset.`,`Disney and Universal sued Midjourney for copying their characters in 2025.`]},"market:bio":{intro:`AI sold to drug makers and biotech labs: models that predict how proteins fold, which genes drive a disease and which molecules might block it, then design new molecules to try. The prize is enormous, since one successful drug can earn billions, but every candidate must still pass years of trials in people.`,how:`Customers usually pay through partnerships: an upfront fee, payments as a candidate passes each milestone, and royalties if it reaches the market. Some AI firms run their own labs and own their drugs outright. Models compete on accuracy against real experiments, so the best data, from labs running thousands of tests a week, matter as much as the code.`,history:`DeepMind's AlphaFold 2 predicted protein structures close to lab accuracy at the CASP14 contest in 2020. Alphabet founded Isomorphic Labs in 2021 to turn AlphaFold into medicines. In 2023 Insilico Medicine began a phase 2 trial of a lung-scarring drug whose target and molecule were both found with AI. In 2024 Isomorphic signed deals with Eli Lilly and Novartis worth up to about $3 billion. AlphaFold 3, released in 2024, also predicted how proteins bind DNA and drug molecules. In 2025 Isomorphic raised $600 million for its own drug programmes.`,facts:[`Bringing one new drug to market cost an estimated $2.6 billion in 2014 (Tufts).`,`Isomorphic's 2024 deals with Lilly and Novartis were worth up to about $3 billion.`]},"market:video":{intro:`Short films made from a sentence or a still picture: clips for adverts, trailers, social media and previews of scenes before a director shoots them. The models learn how things look and move from vast amounts of video.`,how:`A video is many pictures that must stay consistent from frame to frame, so each second costs far more computing than a single image. Users pay subscriptions with credit limits, and studios pay for custom deals. Tools compete on realism, length, sound, physics that look right and control over the camera and characters, while actors and studios fight over likenesses and training footage.`,history:`Runway released Gen-2, one of the first text-to-video tools open to the public, in 2023. OpenAI previewed Sora in February 2024 with minute-long clips that startled Hollywood. In 2024 the producer Tyler Perry paused an $800 million studio expansion after seeing Sora. China's Kuaishou launched its Kling model in June 2024. Google's Veo 3 added generated sound and speech in May 2025. OpenAI's Sora 2 app, a feed of generated clips, topped the US App Store in October 2025.`,facts:[`Tyler Perry paused an $800 million studio expansion in 2024 after seeing Sora.`,`The Sora app reached a million downloads in under five days in 2025.`]},"market:voice":{intro:`Machines that listen and speak: speech recognition that turns calls, meetings and dictation into text, and speech synthesis that reads text aloud in a natural voice. Phone makers, call centres, hospitals, media firms and app developers buy it for assistants, subtitles, dubbing and audiobooks.`,how:`Customers pay per minute of audio transcribed or per character spoken, or license an engine to run inside a device. Neural networks trained on thousands of hours of speech compete on accuracy with accents, noise and specialist words, and on voices that sound human. The big platforms give basic speech away in their phones, so independents win on quality, languages and niches such as medicine.`,history:`In 2012 Google and Microsoft moved their speech recognition onto deep neural networks, cutting errors sharply. Amazon's Echo speaker and its Alexa voice launched in 2014. DeepMind's WaveNet of 2016 generated speech one sound sample at a time and sounded far more natural than older voices. OpenAI released Whisper free in 2022, a recognition model trained on 680,000 hours of audio. Microsoft completed its $19.7 billion purchase of Nuance, whose Dragon software doctors used for notes, in 2022. ElevenLabs, founded in 2022, was valued at $3.3 billion in 2025 for its lifelike synthetic voices.`,facts:[`Microsoft reported a 5.9% word error rate on phone calls in 2016, matching human transcribers.`,`Whisper was trained on 680,000 hours of audio (2022).`,`Microsoft paid $19.7 billion for Nuance in 2022.`]},"market:music":{intro:`Whole songs made from a prompt: a user types a style and a subject, and a model writes the lyrics, sings them and plays the band in a minute or two. Hobbyists, video makers, advertisers and some musicians use it, while record labels guard the recordings it learned from.`,how:`Users pay subscriptions for more songs and the right to use them commercially. Models compete on sound quality, convincing vocals and following the brief. The big question is training: labels say the models copied their recordings, so licences with the labels decide who may sell what, and streaming services must decide whether to pay for machine-made tracks.`,history:`Google showed MusicLM, a research model that turned text into music, in January 2023. In April 2023 a song using AI copies of the voices of Drake and The Weeknd spread online before the label had it removed. Suno released its song generator inside Microsoft Copilot in December 2023. The major labels, through the RIAA, sued Suno and Udio in June 2024. In late 2025 Universal and Warner settled with Udio, and Warner with Suno, in exchange for licensed services.`,facts:[`Suno raised $250 million at a $2.45 billion valuation in 2025.`,`In 2025 Deezer said over a quarter of the tracks uploaded to it were fully AI-made.`]},"market:live":{intro:`Voice assistants you can talk to like a person, in real time: they answer in under a second, can be interrupted mid-sentence, and hear tone, laughter and hesitation, not just words. People use them for conversation practice, tutoring, help on the move and customer calls.`,how:`Older assistants chained three systems: speech to text, a language model, then text to speech, which added seconds of delay and lost the tone of voice. The new ones use one model that takes sound in and speaks sound out. Speed matters most, so smaller fast models win, and every minute of open conversation keeps chips busy, which makes it costly to serve.`,history:`OpenAI showed GPT-4o talking, laughing and translating live in May 2024. Days later in May 2024 the actress Scarlett Johansson objected that its Sky voice sounded like hers, and OpenAI paused it. Google launched Gemini Live in August 2024. Advanced Voice Mode reached paying ChatGPT users in September 2024. In October 2024 OpenAI opened a Realtime API so developers could build their own speech-to-speech apps. In 2025 Gemini Live learned to see through the phone's camera while it talked.`,facts:[`GPT-4o answered spoken questions in about 320 milliseconds on average in 2024, close to human reply times.`,`The Realtime API cost about 6 cents a minute to listen and 24 cents a minute to speak in 2024.`]},"market:world":{intro:`World models generate places you can move through: given a picture or a sentence, the model produces a scene that responds as you walk, drive or act in it. Robot and self-driving car makers use them to train machines on situations too rare or dangerous to film, and game and film makers to build worlds quickly.`,how:`The model predicts the next frames of a world from what has happened and what the player or robot does, while remembering where objects were left. Customers pay for compute time and licences. Models compete on how long a world stays consistent, how believable its physics are, and how fast they run, since an interactive world must keep up with its visitor.`,history:`In 2018 David Ha and Jürgen Schmidhuber's paper World Models trained a game-playing agent inside its own dream of the game. Fei-Fei Li founded World Labs in 2024 with $230 million of funding. In December 2024 Google DeepMind showed Genie 2, which turned a single image into a playable 3D world. NVIDIA released its Cosmos world models for training robots and cars in January 2025. In August 2025 DeepMind's Genie 3 kept a generated world consistent for minutes at 24 frames a second. World Labs released Marble, its first product, in 2025.`,facts:[`Genie 3 generated worlds at 720p and 24 frames a second in 2025.`,`World Labs raised $230 million at its launch in 2024.`]},"market:decide":{intro:`Models that make decisions instead of writing text: they sort a support ticket, flag a payment, route a document or score a sales lead in milliseconds. Each answer is picked from a fixed list and comes with a probability for every option, so a business can choose how sure the model must be before it acts alone.`,how:`A chatbot writes its answer word by word, which is slow and costs money for every word. A decision model reads the input once and scores every allowed answer in a single pass, so it is fast and nearly free to serve. Buyers judge it on accuracy, speed and whether its probabilities are honest: a well-calibrated 90% should be right nine times in ten.`,history:`In 2017 Chuan Guo and colleagues showed that modern neural networks were overconfident, and proposed a simple fix called temperature scaling. Google's BERT of 2018 made it cheap to fine-tune a language model to sort text into categories. In 2023 many companies began asking large chat models for one-word labels, paying for generated text just to get a decision. OpenAI added structured outputs in 2024, forcing a model's reply into a fixed format. In 2026 new services sold calibrated decisions from large models in a single pass, charging only for what was read.`,facts:[`BERT's 2018 release topped eleven language-understanding benchmarks at once.`,`Guo's 2017 study found bigger, deeper networks were worse calibrated than older, smaller ones.`]},"market:robotaxi":{intro:`Taxi rides with nobody in the driver's seat: a passenger books in an app, and a car drives itself through the city to the destination. The operator needs a driving system safe enough for busy streets, a fleet of cars covered in sensors, and permission from regulators city by city.`,how:`The operator keeps the whole fare with no driver to pay, but each car carries costly lidar, cameras and computers, and needs depots, cleaning, charging, maps and remote helpers. Riders choose on price, waiting time and trust. One serious crash can close a service, so safety records and regulators decide who may grow.`,history:`In December 2018 Waymo launched Waymo One, a paid robotaxi service with safety drivers, in the suburbs of Phoenix. In 2020 Waymo opened fully driverless rides to the public there. Baidu's Apollo Go won permits for rides with no one at the wheel in Wuhan and Chongqing in 2022. In August 2023 California let Waymo and Cruise charge for driverless rides around the clock in San Francisco. Cruise lost its permit in October 2023 after one of its cars dragged a pedestrian. Tesla began a small robotaxi service in Austin in June 2025, with a safety monitor on board.`,facts:[`Waymo gave over 250,000 paid rides a week in 2025.`,`Waymo's cars had driven over 100 million miles with no one at the wheel by 2025.`]},"market:devices":{intro:`The main chips inside phones, tablets and laptops: a system-on-chip that packs the processor cores, graphics, camera processor, modem and more onto one piece of silicon. Phone and laptop makers buy them, or design their own, and choose by speed, battery life and price.`,how:`A chip that does more work per watt gives longer battery life and a thinner, cooler device, so being first on the newest manufacturing process matters greatly. Most use processor cores licensed from Arm. Chip firms sell hundreds of millions of units to a few big phone makers, so losing one customer can sink a year, and the richest phone makers design their own chips and buy only the manufacturing.`,history:`Apple put its own A4 chip in the iPad and the iPhone 4 in 2010. In 2017 Apple's A11 Bionic and Huawei's Kirin 970 added neural engines for AI tasks such as recognising faces. US sanctions cut Huawei off from TSMC's factories in 2020. In 2020 Apple also began moving the Mac onto its own M1 chip. In 2023 Huawei's Mate 60 Pro surprised Washington with a 7 nm Kirin chip made in China by SMIC. In 2024 Microsoft's Copilot+ PCs required a neural unit doing 40 trillion operations a second, and chips began to be sold on the AI models they could run on the device.`,facts:[`Apple's M1 held 16 billion transistors in 2020.`,`MediaTek shipped more phone chips than anyone else in 2020 (Counterpoint).`,`Copilot+ PCs needed a neural processor of over 40 trillion operations a second in 2024.`]},"market:accel":{intro:`Chips for the heavy mathematics of science and AI, sold to everyone who fills a data centre: cloud firms, big companies, universities and governments. Graphics chips, first built for games, lead the field because they do thousands of simple sums at the same time.`,how:`Buyers compare performance per dollar and per watt, but software matters as much as silicon: programs written for one maker's tools do not easily move to another's. Chips are sold on boards and in whole servers at high margins. The networking that links thousands of chips into one machine, and the memory beside each chip, decide how big a model can be trained.`,history:`NVIDIA released CUDA in 2007 so that graphics chips could run general programs. In 2012 AlexNet, trained on two NVIDIA gaming cards, won the ImageNet picture-recognition contest by a wide margin. Google revealed in 2016 that it had built its own AI chip, the TPU. NVIDIA's V100 of 2017 added tensor cores for the matrix sums of neural networks. Demand for the H100, launched in 2022, outran supply through 2023. In 2024 NVIDIA briefly became the most valuable company in the world.`,facts:[`AlexNet was trained in 2012 on two GTX 580 cards with 3 GB of memory each.`,`An H100 sold for roughly $25,000 to $40,000 in 2023.`,`NVIDIA's data-centre revenue was $115 billion in its 2025 fiscal year.`]},"market:memory":{intro:`DRAM, the fast working memory in every computer, phone, games console and server. A few giant makers sell it by the billions of chips to device makers, who buy on price, speed and power use, and switch supplier whenever another is cheaper.`,how:`Memory is a commodity: one maker's chip can replace another's, so prices swing with supply. When every maker adds factories at once, prices crash; when demand outruns them, prices soar. Makers compete on cost per bit, which falls with each finer process, and on being first with new standards. Special memory for the fastest processors is much harder to make, so its leader can charge far more.`,history:`Japan's last DRAM maker, Elpida, went bankrupt in 2012. Micron completed its purchase of Elpida in 2013, leaving three big DRAM makers: Samsung, SK hynix and Micron. SK hynix made the first HBM, memory chips stacked in a tower, for AMD's graphics chips in 2013. DRAM sales fell by more than a third in 2019 after a record 2018. In 2024 SK hynix, the main HBM supplier to NVIDIA, earned record profits. In 2025 SK hynix passed Samsung in DRAM revenue for the first time.`,facts:[`Elpida filed for bankruptcy in 2012 with debts of about $5.5 billion.`,`SK hynix overtook Samsung in DRAM revenue in early 2025 (Counterpoint).`]},"market:siwafers":{intro:`The blank discs every chip starts from: thin slices of one huge, almost flawless crystal of silicon, polished flatter than nearly anything else people make. Chipmakers buy them by the million, 300 millimetres across for the newest chips and smaller for older ones.`,how:`Makers melt ultra-pure silicon, dip in a seed crystal and slowly pull up a single-crystal ingot taller than a person, then slice, grind, polish and clean it into wafers. Quality means purity, flatness and very few defects, and qualifying a new supplier takes months, so customers sign long contracts. A handful of firms dominate, and a new plant takes years and billions to build.`,history:`Jan Czochralski discovered his crystal-pulling method in 1916 while studying metals. At Bell Labs in 1950 Gordon Teal and John Little used it to grow single crystals of germanium for transistors. The first factories to use 200 mm wafers opened around 1990. The first 300 mm wafers went into volume production around 2001. GlobalWafers agreed to buy Germany's Siltronic for about €3.75 billion in 2020. The deal collapsed in 2022 when German approval did not arrive in time.`,facts:[`Wafer makers sold a record $13.8 billion of wafers in 2022 (SEMI).`,`Shin-Etsu and SUMCO together made about half the world's wafers in 2022.`]},"market:foundry":{intro:`Factories that make other companies' chips. Designers such as Apple, NVIDIA, AMD and Qualcomm send their layouts to a foundry, which prints them onto wafers and ships back finished chips. It lets a chip company sell the most advanced chips without building a factory that costs billions.`,how:`The foundry with the most advanced process, the smallest transistors using the least power, and the fewest defects wins the biggest customers. Each new process costs tens of billions in factories and tools, so only the largest can keep up. Customers spend years designing for one foundry's process, and moving a chip elsewhere means redesigning it, so trust and on-time delivery keep them loyal.`,history:`Morris Chang founded TSMC in Taiwan in 1987, the first company to make chips only for others. In 2014 TSMC began making Apple's iPhone processors. GlobalFoundries gave up the race to 7 nm in 2018. In 2020 TSMC began volume production at 5 nm, with Apple's chips among the first. A chip shortage in 2021 halted car factories around the world. TSMC began production at its first Arizona fab in late 2024.`,facts:[`TSMC's revenue was about $90 billion in 2024.`,`TSMC took about two thirds of all foundry revenue in 2024 (TrendForce).`]},"market:litho":{intro:`The machines that print chips: they shine light through a mask carrying the circuit pattern and shrink it onto a wafer coated with light-sensitive resist, layer after layer. The most advanced use light of ever shorter wavelengths and cost tens of millions of dollars each.`,how:`Smaller patterns need shorter wavelengths, bigger lenses and near-perfect alignment between layers, so each generation is a huge feat of engineering. Chipmakers buy a few dozen machines a year at enormous prices and pay for service contracts for their whole lives. The market belongs to whoever can build the best machine at all, and export rules decide who may buy it.`,history:`Philips and ASM International founded ASML in 1984, in a leaky shed beside a Philips office in Eindhoven. ASML shipped its first two EUV demonstration machines to research labs in 2006. Intel, TSMC and Samsung invested in ASML in 2012 to speed up EUV. Samsung and TSMC began making chips with EUV in 2019. In 2019 the Dutch government held back a licence to export an EUV machine to China. In December 2023 ASML shipped its first High-NA EUV machine to Intel.`,facts:[`EUV light has a wavelength of 13.5 nm, against 193 nm for older tools (2019).`,`A High-NA EUV machine cost about €350 million in 2023.`]},"market:rent":{intro:`Renting graphics chips by the hour to people who need lots of computing but own no data centre: startups, researchers, film studios and AI labs. The renter gets a powerful server over the internet; the owner keeps the chips busy and earns back what they cost.`,how:`Prices are set per chip per hour, with discounts for long contracts. The economics turn on keeping costly chips busy and power cheap: an idle chip earns nothing while it ages. Renters pick on price, on which chips are free right now, and on how fast the network links them, since big training jobs need thousands of chips working as one.`,history:`Amazon added servers with NVIDIA graphics chips to its cloud in 2010. CoreWeave began in 2017 as Atlantic Crypto, a firm mining the Ethereum currency with graphics cards. When Ethereum stopped mining in 2022, its GPU farms turned to renting chips out. The ChatGPT boom made H100 chips scarce in 2023, and Microsoft agreed to spend billions renting capacity from CoreWeave. CoreWeave listed on the stock market in March 2025. OpenAI's contracts with CoreWeave added up to about $22 billion by 2025.`,facts:[`Amazon's first GPU cloud servers of 2010 used NVIDIA Tesla chips.`,`OpenAI signed about $22 billion of contracts with CoreWeave in 2025.`]},"market:satnet":{intro:`Internet access from satellites, for places cables do not reach: ships, aircraft, oil rigs, farms, villages and disaster zones. A dish on the roof talks to a satellite, which relays the data to a ground station linked to the internet.`,how:`A geostationary satellite hangs 36,000 km up and covers a third of the Earth, but each signal takes about half a second there and back. Satellites in low orbit fly only a few hundred kilometres up, so they are quick, but a service needs thousands of them. Customers pay for a dish and a monthly plan; the operator's costs are building and launching satellites, so cheap rockets decide who can afford a fleet.`,history:`Hughes launched DirecPC, satellite internet for homes, in 1996. ViaSat-1, launched in 2011, carried more capacity than all the satellites then serving North America put together. SpaceX launched its first 60 Starlink satellites in May 2019. OneWeb went bankrupt in 2020 and was rescued by the UK government and India's Bharti. In 2022 Starlink dishes kept Ukraine connected after the Russian invasion, and the service passed a million customers that December. Amazon launched its first production Kuiper satellites in 2025.`,facts:[`Starlink had over 6,000 satellites in orbit by 2024.`,`Starlink passed 6 million customers in 2025.`]},"market:quantum":{intro:`Time on quantum computers, rented over the cloud by chemists, materials scientists, banks and researchers who want to learn to use them. Quantum bits, or qubits, can hold a blend of 0 and 1, so in principle some problems could be solved far faster than any ordinary computer can manage.`,how:`Qubits are fragile: heat, noise and stray signals make them lose their state in a fraction of a second, so reliable work needs error correction from many physical qubits per useful one. Customers pay per hour or per run, or join paid research networks, mostly to prepare for the day the machines become useful. Makers compete on qubit count, error rates and how long their qubits last.`,history:`In 1981 Richard Feynman proposed computers built from quantum parts to simulate nature. In 1994 Peter Shor showed that a quantum computer could break the encryption that protects the internet. In 2016 IBM put a five-qubit computer on the cloud, free for anyone to program. In 2019 Google said its 53-qubit Sycamore did in 200 seconds a task that would take a supercomputer 10,000 years. IonQ listed on the stock market in 2021. In December 2024 Google's Willow chip showed errors falling as more qubits were added.`,facts:[`IBM's Condor chip of 2023 had 1,121 qubits.`,`Google's Willow chip of 2024 had 105 qubits.`]},"market:dataplat":{intro:`Software for storing and crunching everything a company records, clicks, sales, sensor readings and logs, on clusters of cheap servers working together. The core programs are free and open source, so vendors sell support, management tools and hosted versions.`,how:`Instead of one big expensive machine, data is split across hundreds of ordinary servers, and each processes its own share at the same time. Companies pay subscriptions per server or per amount of computing for a packaged, supported version. The free core makes it easy to switch vendors, so firms compete on extra features and on running it for the customer in the cloud.`,history:`Doug Cutting and Mike Cafarella made Hadoop, modelled on papers Google had published, a separate open-source project in 2006. Yahoo ran Hadoop on about 10,000 processor cores by 2008. Cloudera was founded in 2008 to sell Hadoop support, and Hortonworks spun out of Yahoo in 2011. The Berkeley creators of Spark founded Databricks in 2013. Cloudera and Hortonworks merged in January 2019. Databricks raised $10 billion at a $62 billion valuation in 2024.`,facts:[`Hortonworks' revenue grew from $46 million in 2014 to $262 million in 2017.`,`Databricks was valued at $62 billion in 2024.`]},"market:observability":{intro:`Tools that collect the logs, measurements and traces every server and program gives off, so engineers can see what is happening and find what broke. When a website slows down at three in the morning, this is where the engineer on call looks first.`,how:`Customers usually pay by the amount of data they send in, per gigabyte or per server watched. Data only grows as companies add servers and services, so bills grow too, and customers keep looking for ways to collect less. Vendors compete on how fast they search and raise alerts, how many systems they connect to, and how many separate tools they replace.`,history:`Splunk was founded in San Francisco in 2003 to make machine logs searchable like the web. New Relic was founded in 2008 to watch how web applications performed. Splunk listed on the stock market in 2012. Datadog, a New York startup that watched servers from the cloud, listed in 2019. In 2023 Francisco Partners and TPG bought New Relic for about $6.5 billion. Cisco completed its purchase of Splunk for about $28 billion in 2024.`,facts:[`Splunk's revenue grew from $121 million in fiscal 2012 to $3.65 billion in fiscal 2023.`,`Datadog's revenue was $2.68 billion in 2024.`,`Cisco paid about $28 billion for Splunk in 2024.`]},"market:cloudnative":{intro:`Everything between a developer's code and the cloud's machines: platforms that run an app with one command, containers that package a program with everything it needs, cluster managers that schedule thousands of them, and tools that describe servers as code. The core is mostly free; companies pay for support, management and hosted versions.`,how:`Developers adopt free tools first, and the companies that employ them then buy support, security and a supported distribution. Cloud providers offer the same software as a managed service, which squeezes the independent vendors. Products compete on reliability, ease of use and how many developers already know them, so open-source popularity is the main weapon.`,history:`Salesforce bought Heroku, which ran a developer's app with a single command, for $212 million in 2010. Docker was released as open source in 2013 and made containers easy. Google released Kubernetes in 2014, based on Borg, the system that ran its own data centres. The Cloud Native Computing Foundation was set up in 2015 to look after Kubernetes. IBM bought Red Hat, maker of the OpenShift Kubernetes platform, for $34 billion in 2019. IBM completed its $6.4 billion purchase of HashiCorp in 2025.`,facts:[`Salesforce paid $212 million for Heroku in 2010.`,`IBM paid $34 billion for Red Hat in 2019.`]},"market:embedded":{intro:`Operating systems for the computers hidden inside things: factory robots, telephone exchanges, printers, engines, satellites and medical devices. Many must respond within a guaranteed time, a few millionths of a second, every single time, so they are called real-time systems.`,how:`Device makers license an operating system for a fee per unit shipped, from a few cents to many dollars, plus tools and support. Systems for aircraft, cars, trains and medical devices must be certified as safe, which takes years and fetches high prices. Once a product line is built on one system its maker rarely changes, so a design win lasts a decade.`,history:`QNX, a small real-time system for PCs, was first released in 1982 by two graduates of the University of Waterloo. California's Wind River Systems released VxWorks in 1987. VxWorks ran NASA's Mars Pathfinder lander in 1997. Research In Motion, maker of the BlackBerry, bought QNX in 2010. The Linux Foundation launched the free Zephyr system in 2016, and Amazon took over the free FreeRTOS in 2017. Aptiv bought Wind River for $4.3 billion in 2022.`,facts:[`VxWorks controlled NASA's Mars Pathfinder lander in 1997.`,`Aptiv paid $4.3 billion for Wind River in 2022.`]},"market:autosw":{intro:`All the software a car runs: engine and brake controllers, airbags, dashboards, entertainment screens and driver assistance. Carmakers buy most of it from their suppliers, packed inside dozens of small computers called electronic control units, each with its own job.`,how:`Suppliers such as Bosch and Continental sell each control unit with its software at a price per car, while specialists license operating systems, tools and standard software layers. Safety rules demand strict testing, and a model's software is fixed for years, so contracts are long. As cars gain more code, carmakers want to own the software and update it over the air like a phone.`,history:`From 1996 every new car sold in the United States had to carry the OBD-II diagnostic port. General Motors launched OnStar, a connected service with a button for help, in 1996. In 2003 BMW, Bosch, Continental and others founded AUTOSAR to standardise car software. Tesla began updating its Model S over the air in 2012. Volkswagen set up its own software unit in 2020, renamed CARIAD in 2021. In 2024 Volkswagen agreed to invest up to $5.8 billion in a software joint venture with Rivian.`,facts:[`IEEE Spectrum estimated a premium car ran about 100 million lines of code in 2009.`,`McKinsey valued automotive software at about $31 billion in 2019.`]},"market:transistors":{intro:`Single transistors, tiny switches and amplifiers made from germanium or silicon, sold one by one to the makers of radios, hearing aids, military equipment and computers. A transistor does the job of a vacuum tube while using a fraction of the power and lasting far longer.`,how:`Makers grow crystals, add traces of impurities to form layers, cut them into tiny pieces and seal each one in a metal can. Early yields are poor and prices high, so the armed forces pay most at first; as factories learn, prices fall year after year and new uses open. Buyers choose on speed, reliability, price and whether a part survives heat and cold.`,history:`Bell Labs licensed the transistor to other companies for $25,000 each in 1952. Hearing aids were the first products to use transistors, on sale from late 1952. In 1954 Texas Instruments made the first silicon transistor and supplied the parts for the Regency TR-1, the first transistor radio. Sony's TR-55 of 1955 was Japan's first transistor radio. Fairchild's planar process of 1959 made transistors flat, cheap and reliable. By 1975 most transistors were made by the thousand inside integrated circuits, and single ones became a cheap commodity part.`,facts:[`A Bell Labs transistor licence cost $25,000 in 1952.`,`The Regency TR-1 radio sold for $49.95 in 1954.`,`US transistor sales reached about $300 million in 1960.`]},"market:ics":{intro:`Integrated circuits put several transistors, resistors and the wiring between them on a single chip of silicon, so a whole circuit is made in one go instead of soldered together from parts. The first ones hold a handful of components and are sold as logic gates and amplifiers.`,how:`The pattern is printed onto a silicon wafer with light and chemicals, so hundreds of circuits are made at once. At first yields are low and a chip costs far more than the parts it replaces, so only missiles and spacecraft pay, where weight and reliability are worth any price. As volumes grow, prices fall fast, and computer makers and then consumer products take over.`,history:`Jack Kilby built the first integrated circuit at Texas Instruments in 1958, and Robert Noyce at Fairchild designed a practical planar version in 1959. Fairchild and Texas Instruments began selling chips in 1961. By 1963 the Apollo and Minuteman missile programmes were buying most of the chips made in America. In 1965 Gordon Moore predicted that the number of components on a chip would double every year. In 1968 Noyce and Moore left Fairchild to found Intel. By 1975 a chip could hold thousands of transistors, and the business split into memory, microprocessors and logic.`,facts:[`Kilby's first circuit of 1958 was a sliver of germanium with one transistor.`,`Moore's 1965 article predicted 65,000 components on a chip by 1975.`]},"market:micro":{intro:`A microprocessor puts a computer's whole processing unit on one chip: it fetches instructions from memory, does the sums and moves the data. Makers of calculators, terminals, cash registers, machines and small computers buy it instead of designing circuits of their own.`,how:`Programs are written for one instruction set, so a chip family that gathers software locks its customers in. Each generation packs more transistors and runs faster for the same price, and speed per dollar decides most sales. Makers that own their factories can push their chips faster, while rivals license a design and rely on someone else's production.`,history:`Intel's 4004, designed for the Japanese calculator maker Busicom, went on sale in 1971 with 2,300 transistors. MOS Technology's 6502 sold for $25 in 1975, a fraction of rivals' prices. In 1977 the 6502 powered both the Apple II and the Commodore PET. IBM chose Intel's 8088 for its PC in 1981, which made the x86 family the standard for desktops. AMD's Athlon beat Intel to 1 gigahertz in 2000. By 2010 chips built on Arm designs ran almost every mobile phone.`,facts:[`The 4004 of 1971 had 2,300 transistors and ran at 740 kilohertz.`,`Intel's revenue grew from $855 million in 1980 to $33.7 billion in 2000.`,`Intel's Pentium 4 reached 3.8 gigahertz in 2004.`]},"market:gfx":{intro:`Chips that draw 3D graphics for games: they take a scene of triangles and textures and paint millions of coloured pixels a second, so a game world can move smoothly. They are sold to the makers of add-in cards and computers, or built onto the computer's main board.`,how:`A new generation arrives every year or so, roughly doubling speed, so a maker that slips a cycle loses the gamers who buy the top chips and the reviews that sell the cheaper ones. Game developers write for the most popular features, which drives the next upgrade. Margins come from the fastest chips; the volume is in cheap graphics built into ordinary PCs.`,history:`S3's ViRGE of 1995 was one of the first cheap 3D chips, so slow that critics called it a decelerator. NVIDIA, founded in 1993, launched its first chip, the NV1, in 1995, and it flopped. NVIDIA launched the GeForce 256 in 1999, calling it the first GPU. 3dfx collapsed in 2000, and NVIDIA bought its assets. AMD bought ATI for $5.4 billion in 2006. By 2010 Intel was building graphics into its processors and shipping more graphics than anyone.`,facts:[`AMD paid $5.4 billion for ATI in 2006.`,`NVIDIA's revenue was about $3.5 billion in its 2011 fiscal year.`]},"market:dram":{intro:`DRAM chips are the main working memory of computers: each bit is a tiny electric charge held in a capacitor that must be refreshed many times a second. They are sold by the million to computer makers, at a price per bit that falls year after year.`,how:`Every maker's chips follow the same standards, so customers buy on price and memory behaves like a commodity. Each new generation holds four times the bits; the first to make it in volume earns fat margins before everyone else catches up. Makers build factories in booms, which brings gluts and crashes, and only the deepest pockets survive the bad years.`,history:`Intel's 1103 of 1970 was the first DRAM sold in volume, and it began replacing magnetic core memory. Mostek's 16-kilobit MK4116 of 1976 became the industry standard. Japanese makers such as NEC, Hitachi and Toshiba won the 64-kilobit generation in the early 1980s and drove prices down. Intel quit DRAM in 1985. Samsung became the world's biggest DRAM maker in 1992. Prices crashed in 1996 as new factories flooded the market.`,facts:[`The Intel 1103 held 1,024 bits in 1970.`,`DRAM sales reached about $40 billion in 1995.`,`DRAM sales were about $39.6 billion in 2010 (iSuppli).`]},"market:flash":{intro:`Flash memory keeps its data without power, like a disk, but has no moving parts: bits are trapped as charges in tiny cells and erased a whole block at a time, in a flash. It holds the start-up code inside computers and gadgets, and anything small that must not forget.`,how:`There are two kinds: NOR flash reads quickly and holds program code, while NAND packs bits more densely and suits files. Like other memory it is sold on price per bit, and prices swing with factory capacity. Each generation packs more bits into each cell, so the price per bit falls fast and new uses keep opening, from cameras to music players and phones.`,history:`Toshiba's Fujio Masuoka presented flash memory in 1984. Masuoka's team presented NAND flash, denser and cheaper per bit, in 1987. Intel sold the first commercial NOR flash chip in 1988. CompactFlash cards for digital cameras appeared in 1994. Apple's iPod nano switched from a tiny hard disk to flash in 2005, and Apple paid $1.25 billion in advance to lock up supply. By 2012 NAND sold about $20 billion a year, with Samsung and Toshiba making two thirds of it.`,facts:[`Intel's first flash chip of 1988 held 256 kilobits.`,`Apple prepaid $1.25 billion for flash memory in 2005.`]},"market:disks":{intro:`Hard disks store data magnetically on spinning metal platters, read and written by heads that float a hair's breadth above the surface. Any piece of data can be reached in a fraction of a second, unlike tape, and the bits pack tighter every year, so a byte keeps getting cheaper to keep.`,how:`Drives are sold to computer makers in large orders, so drive makers fight fiercely on capacity and price per byte. Each new, smaller size of drive opens a new kind of computer, and firms that miss a size often die. Margins are thin, makers keep merging, and the survivors are those that make their own heads and platters.`,history:`IBM shipped the 350 disk unit with its RAMAC computer in 1956: fifty 24-inch platters holding 5 million characters. IBM's 3340 Winchester drive of 1973 sealed the heads and disks together in one clean box. Seagate's ST-506 of 1980 brought a 5-megabyte disk to small computers. IBM's giant magnetoresistive read heads of 1997 multiplied how much a disk could hold. Floods in Thailand, where many drives were made, cut supply and doubled disk prices in 2011. By 2012 mergers had left only three disk makers: Seagate, Western Digital and Toshiba.`,facts:[`The IBM 350 of 1956 weighed about a tonne and stored 5 million characters.`,`About 22 million disk drives were sold in 1989, worth $23 billion.`,`About 650 million hard disks were shipped in 2010.`]},"market:ssds":{intro:`Solid-state drives are flash memory chips dressed up as a disk: they plug in where a hard drive goes, but have no moving parts. They find a file in a tenth of a millisecond instead of several milliseconds, start a computer in seconds and survive being dropped.`,how:`An SSD is a stack of flash chips and a controller, a small processor that spreads the writing evenly so the flash wears out slowly. Its price follows flash prices, which fall fast but stay far above disks per gigabyte, so buyers pay for speed, not space. Laptop makers want them for thinness and battery life, and data centres for speed.`,history:`Samsung began selling 32 GB flash drives for laptops in 2006. Apple's first MacBook Air offered a 64 GB solid-state drive in 2008 as a $999 option. Intel's X25-M of 2008 showed how much a good controller mattered. About 11 million SSDs were sold in 2009. Apple made flash standard in the MacBook Air in 2010. By 2012 SSDs were the rule in thin laptops, which Intel branded ultrabooks.`,facts:[`The MacBook Air's 64 GB SSD option cost $999 in 2008.`,`Intel's X25-M read data at 250 megabytes a second in 2008.`,`About 17 million SSDs were sold in 2011.`]},"market:mainframes":{intro:`Large central computers that fill a room and serve a whole organisation: they run payroll, bank accounts, insurance policies, stock records and census counts. Most are rented from the maker with service included, and once a customer's programs are written for one, it rarely leaves.`,how:`Makers lease machines by the month, with engineers, software and training included, so the customer is tied in for years. New models must run the old programs, so a maker that keeps its range compatible keeps its customers. Rivals win by being cheaper, faster, or able to run the leader's programs.`,history:`Remington Rand delivered the first UNIVAC I to the US Census Bureau in 1951. IBM's 650, announced in 1953, sold nearly 2,000 machines and made IBM the leader. In 1964 IBM announced the System/360, a family of compatible machines from small to large. Gene Amdahl's company began selling cheaper machines that ran IBM's programs unchanged in 1975. IBM's zSeries of 2000 brought Linux to the mainframe. In 2012 IBM's zEC12 still ran the core accounts of many of the world's big banks.`,facts:[`IBM spent about $5 billion developing the System/360 (1964).`,`The first UNIVAC I of 1951 used about 5,200 vacuum tubes.`]},"market:supercomputers":{intro:`The fastest computers on Earth, built for the biggest sums: designing nuclear weapons, forecasting the weather, breaking codes, finding oil and simulating physics. Each is a showpiece, and only a few are sold each year, to national laboratories, weather services and universities.`,how:`Buyers pay millions for raw speed, measured in floating-point operations per second, and each machine is often custom-built with its own cooling. Governments are the main customers, so budgets and prestige decide sales more than profit. Makers win by being fastest; a year behind and the buyers go elsewhere.`,history:`Seymour Cray's CDC 6600 of 1964 was the fastest computer in the world, about three times faster than the record holder before it. Cray left to found Cray Research, whose Cray-1 went to Los Alamos in 1976. The Cray-2 of 1985 was cooled by bathing its circuits in liquid. Japan's Earth Simulator topped the list in 2002 and alarmed American planners. IBM's Roadrunner passed a petaflop, a thousand trillion sums a second, in 2008. Japan's K computer passed 10 petaflops in 2011.`,facts:[`About 100 CDC 6600s were sold after its 1964 launch.`,`A Cray-1 cost about $8 million in 1976.`,`Roadrunner reached 1.026 petaflops in 2008.`]},"market:minis":{intro:`Minicomputers are smaller, cheaper computers for one laboratory, factory or department rather than a whole company. They fit in a cabinet or two, cost a fraction of a mainframe, and the scientists and engineers who use them can sit at the console themselves.`,how:`Makers sell to engineers who write their own programs, and to other firms that build the minicomputer into a product, such as a typesetting or laboratory system, and resell it. Low prices, small size and easy connection to instruments win sales. Each maker's programs run only on its own machines, so customers stay loyal while the machines keep getting cheaper.`,history:`DEC's PDP-8 of 1965 sold for $18,000 and launched the minicomputer market. Data General, founded by former DEC engineers, shipped its Nova in 1969. DEC's PDP-11 appeared in 1970 and became the best-selling minicomputer. DEC's VAX-11/780 of 1977 was powerful enough to run whole departments. Ken Olsen left DEC in 1992 after heavy losses, as workstations and networked PCs took its customers. Compaq bought DEC in 1998.`,facts:[`A PDP-8 cost $18,000 in 1965.`,`About 100 companies made minicomputers in 1970.`,`Compaq paid $9.6 billion for DEC in 1998.`]},"market:calculators":{intro:`Electronic machines that add, subtract, multiply and divide, replacing mechanical adding machines and slide rules. The first ones are desk-sized and cost as much as a small car; every model is smaller and cheaper than the last.`,how:`A calculator is a few chips that do the arithmetic, a keyboard and a display, so its price falls with the price of chips. Makers compete on size, battery life, features and price, and a newcomer with a cheaper chip can wipe out an incumbent in a season. Scientific and programmable models for engineers and students fetch higher prices and more loyalty.`,history:`Britain's ANITA of 1961, built with vacuum tubes, was the first electronic desk calculator. Sharp's CS-10A of 1964 was an early all-transistor model, priced like a small car. In 1971 Busicom's order for calculator chips led Intel to make the 4004. Hewlett-Packard's HP-35 of 1972, the first pocket scientific calculator, cost $395. Busicom went bankrupt in 1974 as prices collapsed, and Bowmar, an early leader in pocket calculators, followed in 1975. Texas Instruments' TI-81 of 1990 made the graphing calculator a classroom standard.`,facts:[`The HP-35 cost $395 in 1972.`,`Casio's Mini of 1972 sold over a million units in its first ten months.`]},"market:pcs":{intro:`A computer of one's own, small enough for a desk and cheap enough for a person or a small business to buy. People use them for games, programming, writing, sums and accounts, and for keeping records that once needed a filing cabinet.`,how:`Most makers buy chips, disks, keyboards and software from others and assemble them, so the big money goes to whoever owns the standard parts that everything else must fit. Buyers want the software they need, a trusted brand and a low price. Once machines share a standard, makers compete mostly on price, and margins shrink.`,history:`The MITS Altair 8800 kit, sold for $439 in 1975, launched the hobby market. In 1977 the Apple II, Commodore PET and TRS-80 brought ready-built computers to homes. IBM's PC of 1981 set the standard for business. Compaq's portable of 1982 showed that clones could run IBM's software. Laptops outsold desktops for the first time in 2008. World sales peaked at about 350 million in 2011, then slipped in 2012 as tablets and phones took their place.`,facts:[`The Altair 8800 kit cost $439 in 1975.`,`IBM's first PC cost $1,565 in 1981.`,`About 59.7 million PCs were sold in 1995.`]},"market:workstations":{intro:`Workstations are powerful desktop computers for engineers, scientists and designers: a fast processor, a large sharp screen, a network connection and the Unix operating system. They design chips, cars and aircraft, model molecules and draw special effects for films.`,how:`Makers sell at high prices to firms that need speed, and court software vendors to bring design programs to their machines. Each maker builds its own processor and version of Unix, so whoever has the fastest chip for the price and the key design programs wins. When cheaper personal computers catch up in speed, the workstation's premium vanishes.`,history:`Apollo Computer shipped its first networked workstation, the DN100, in 1981. Sun Microsystems and Silicon Graphics were both founded in 1982: Sun built workstations on standard Unix and networking, and SGI built them for 3D graphics. HP bought Apollo in 1989. Silicon Graphics machines rendered the dinosaurs of Jurassic Park in 1993. Workstation sales reached about $11.5 billion in 1996, led by Sun. Silicon Graphics filed for bankruptcy in 2006 as cheap PCs with graphics cards took its work.`,facts:[`Sun's name stood for Stanford University Network (1982).`,`Workstations sold about $6 billion in 1989, Sun taking 29% (Dataquest).`]},"market:servers":{intro:`Servers are computers that serve other computers: they store a company's files, handle its mail and databases, and answer requests from across the network. They run day and night in back rooms and data centres, built for reliability rather than for a person to sit at.`,how:`Buyers compare price, reliability, speed and the support behind them, and buy more as their networks grow. Servers built from PC parts and running Windows or Linux compete with costlier Unix machines that use their own processors. The winners sell many cheap standard boxes; the margins move to software and service.`,history:`Microsoft's Windows NT of 1993 gave servers built from PC parts a serious operating system. Server sales ran at about $60 billion a year in 2000. Google revealed in 2009 that it built its own cheap servers, each with a battery inside. Oracle bought Sun Microsystems for $7.4 billion in 2010. Facebook launched the Open Compute Project in 2011 to share its own server designs. About 8 million servers were sold in 2012.`,facts:[`IDC counted 7 million servers sold in 2005, worth $51.3 billion.`,`Oracle paid $7.4 billion for Sun Microsystems in 2010.`]},"market:pdas":{intro:`Personal digital assistants are pocket computers for a diary, address book, notes and to-do lists, written on with a stylus. They connect to a desk computer so that the same data travels with their owner.`,how:`Makers sell to business people and gadget lovers at a few hundred dollars each. The device that is smallest, fastest and simplest to use wins, along with the number of add-on programs. Handwriting recognition makes or breaks a model, and a cheap price matters less than reliability, since people trust it with their whole diary.`,history:`Apple launched the Newton MessagePad in 1993 at $699, and its handwriting recognition was widely mocked. Palm's Pilot of 1996 succeeded with a simple shorthand alphabet called Graffiti. Apple ended the Newton in 1998. Microsoft's Pocket PC arrived in 2000, the year Palm held about three quarters of American sales. World sales fell to about 9 million in 2004 as phones gained the same features. By 2009 smartphones had all but replaced the separate organiser.`,facts:[`The Newton MessagePad cost $699 in 1993.`,`Palm sold about a million Pilots within 18 months of its 1996 launch.`]},"market:phones":{intro:`Mobile phones people can carry: handsets that connect by radio to towers spread across a city. The first are heavy bricks costing thousands of dollars, used by business people and the rich; each generation is smaller, cheaper and lasts longer on a charge.`,how:`Handset makers sell to the phone networks, which often discount phones in return for a contract, and to shops. Buyers choose on size, battery life, design and price. As phones get cheaper they become fashion items sold by the hundreds of millions, so scale in the factories, design and brand decide the winners.`,history:`Motorola's DynaTAC 8000X, the first handheld mobile phone sold, went on sale in 1983 for $3,995. Motorola's StarTAC of 1996 was the first flip phone and weighed 88 grams. Nokia passed Motorola as the biggest phone maker in 1998. Nokia's simple 1100 of 2003 sold about 250 million units, more than any other phone. Gartner counted about 1.2 billion handsets sold in 2008. Samsung passed Nokia as the biggest phone maker in 2012.`,facts:[`The DynaTAC 8000X weighed about 790 grams and cost $3,995 in 1983.`,`About 108 million handsets were sold in 1997.`,`About 1.75 billion handsets were sold in 2012.`]},"market:smartphones":{intro:`Phones that are also small computers: they handle e-mail, browse the web and run programs their owners add. At first they are bulky, with tiny keyboards or styluses, and are sold mostly to business people.`,how:`Makers win by combining a good phone with a good operating system and by drawing developers to write programs for it. Phone networks sell them with data plans and subsidies. Battery life, the screen, the keyboard and the software all matter, and a maker whose software falls behind can lose its customers within a year or two.`,history:`Ericsson's R380 of 2000 was the first phone sold as a smartphone, running the Symbian system. Research In Motion's BlackBerry 5810 added phone calls to its e-mail device in 2002. Apple's iPhone of 2007 replaced the keys with a multi-touch screen. Google's Android arrived on the HTC Dream in 2008. Nokia dropped Symbian for Microsoft's Windows Phone in 2011. In 2012 Samsung and Apple sold about half of all smartphones.`,facts:[`The first iPhone cost $499 for 4 GB in 2007.`,`About 297 million smartphones were sold in 2010 (Gartner).`]},"market:progsvc":{intro:`Companies that write custom programs for clients: governments, armed forces, banks, insurers and even the computer makers themselves. There is no product, only skilled people billed by the hour: the first software business of all.`,how:`Firms win contracts by bidding on projects, often for the government, and earn a mark-up on programmers' hours. Growth means hiring and training more programmers, so margins stay modest and the business rises and falls with big contracts. Clients keep the firm that knows their systems, and a big project that runs late can bankrupt a small firm.`,history:`Computer Usage Company was founded in New York in 1955, the first firm to sell programming. The RAND Corporation spun off System Development Corporation in 1957 to program the SAGE air-defence system, and it trained hundreds of the first programmers. Ross Perot founded Electronic Data Systems in 1962. In 1970 Americans spent about $650 million on contract programming, against $70 million on packaged software. General Motors bought EDS for $2.5 billion in 1984. By 1995 the business was called IT services, and India's Infosys and Tata Consultancy Services were growing fast.`,facts:[`Ross Perot founded EDS with $1,000 in 1962.`,`General Motors paid $2.5 billion for EDS in 1984.`]},"market:netos":{intro:`Software that turns one PC on an office network into a server: it keeps everyone's files in one place, shares the printers and controls who may see what. It lets a small office share data the way a big company does with its mainframe.`,how:`Vendors sell server software licensed by the number of users, along with network cards and training. Engineers certified on one system recommend it to their next employer, so the leader keeps its offices. The danger is that the makers of desktop and server operating systems build networking in for free.`,history:`Novell shipped NetWare in 1983. By 1990 NetWare held about 70% of the market for network operating systems. Microsoft's Windows NT of 1993 built file and print sharing into the operating system. Novell bought WordPerfect in 1994, a distraction just as NT gained ground. Novell bought SuSE Linux in 2004 to find a new future. By 2005 Windows servers had taken most of NetWare's offices.`,facts:[`Novell sold WordPerfect to Corel in 1996, two years after buying it.`,`Novell paid $210 million for SuSE Linux in 2004.`]},"market:alm":{intro:`Tools for managing big software projects: diagramming the design, tracking requirements, testing, keeping versions and logging bugs. The buyers are usually the managers, who want to know where a project stands, rather than the programmers.`,how:`Vendors sell to large companies and government agencies by the seat, with consulting, promising fewer late and failed projects. The tools work best when a whole department adopts them, so a vendor that ties design, testing and tracking together locks the customer in. Simple, cheap tools that teams can start using on their own keep undercutting the heavy suites.`,history:`In 1985 computer-aided software engineering tools such as Excelerator promised to draw a system's design and generate its code. In 1997 Rational Software's Grady Booch, Ivar Jacobson and James Rumbaugh produced the Unified Modeling Language for drawing software designs. Seventeen programmers wrote the Agile Manifesto in 2001, turning many teams against heavy process tools. Atlassian launched its Jira bug tracker in 2002, sold cheaply online. IBM bought Rational for $2.1 billion in 2003. HP bought the testing leader of the day for $4.5 billion in 2006.`,facts:[`IBM paid $2.1 billion for Rational in 2003.`,`HP paid $4.5 billion for the leading maker of testing tools in 2006.`,`Gartner put the market at $1.26 billion in 2007.`]},"market:codehost":{intro:`Websites where programmers keep their code, track bugs and review each other's changes before they are accepted. Open-source projects get space for free, so strangers can study and improve their code; companies pay to keep their code private and to have it tested automatically.`,how:`Hosts grow by being where the open-source projects are, because every programmer who joins one gets an account. Money comes from businesses paying per user for private code, and from versions installed on their own servers. A host that makes working together easy draws the world's developers, who then bring it into their workplaces.`,history:`VA Linux launched SourceForge in 1999 to host open-source projects for free. Google opened its own Project Hosting in 2006. GitHub launched in 2008, built around the Git version-control system and its pull requests for proposing changes. Atlassian bought Bitbucket in 2010. GitLab began as an open-source project in Ukraine in 2011, letting companies run a code host on their own servers. Microsoft bought GitHub for $7.5 billion in 2018.`,facts:[`GitHub raised $100 million from Andreessen Horowitz in 2012.`,`GitHub earned about $300 million a year when Microsoft bought it in 2018.`]},"market:virt":{intro:`Software that cuts one physical server into many virtual machines, each running its own operating system as if it had the hardware to itself. Companies use it to fill servers that would otherwise sit mostly idle, and to set up a new server in minutes instead of weeks.`,how:`A thin layer called a hypervisor sits between the hardware and the operating systems and shares out processors, memory and disks. Vendors license it per processor and sell management tools on top, which is where the margins lie. Data centres save on machines, power and space, so the case sells itself; free hypervisors push vendors to compete on management software.`,history:`IBM ran virtual machines on its mainframes from 1972 with VM/370. VMware, founded in 1998, shipped VMware Workstation in 1999, bringing virtual machines to ordinary PC chips. The free Xen hypervisor came out of Cambridge University in 2003. EMC bought VMware for $635 million in 2004. Amazon's cloud rented Xen virtual machines from 2006. Microsoft gave away Hyper-V with Windows Server 2008.`,facts:[`EMC paid $635 million for VMware in 2004.`,`VMware earned $3.77 billion in 2011.`,`Broadcom bought VMware for about $69 billion in 2023.`]},"market:codecs":{intro:`The licensing of the formats that squeeze sound and pictures into small files: MPEG video and audio, Dolby sound. Every player, television decoder and computer that uses them pays a small royalty per device to the companies that own the patents.`,how:`Patent owners pool their patents, often through a licensing administrator, and charge from a few cents to a few dollars per device, so huge volumes add up to steady income at almost no cost. A format becomes a must-have when broadcasters, disc makers or phone networks adopt it, and then every maker must pay. A free format that is good enough can shrink the fees.`,history:`In 1992 the MPEG committee approved MPEG-1, which included the MP3 audio format developed largely at Germany's Fraunhofer Institute. Dolby Digital sound reached cinemas in 1992 with Batman Returns. MPEG LA was set up in 1996 to license MPEG-2 patents for DVDs and digital television. H.264, approved in 2003, became the video format of Blu-ray, YouTube and the iPhone. Google bought the codec maker On2 in 2010 and released its VP8 format free. The MP3 patents expired in 2017, and Fraunhofer ended its licensing programme.`,facts:[`Dolby earned about $807 million from licensing in its 2013 fiscal year.`,`Fraunhofer earned about €100 million from MP3 licences in its best year (2000s).`]},"market:archivers":{intro:`Programs that squeeze files to half their size or less, so they fit on floppy disks and travel faster over slow modems, and that bundle many files into one archive. Many are shareware: free to try, with users asked to pay if they keep using it.`,how:`Compression finds repeated patterns in data and writes them more briefly, then rebuilds the original exactly. The format everyone can open wins, because people send archives to each other. Money comes from registrations and business licences, and from disk-doubling tools that squeeze a whole hard disk. Anything the operating system does for free takes away the reason to buy.`,history:`System Enhancement Associates released ARC in 1985. After SEA sued Phil Katz over his faster PKARC in 1988, Katz created a new format, ZIP, released with PKZIP in 1989. WinZip of 1991 put ZIP inside a Windows window. Microsoft built disk compression into MS-DOS as DoubleSpace in 1993, and Stac, maker of the Stacker disk doubler, won $120 million from it in court in 1994. Windows XP opened ZIP files by itself from 2001. Corel bought WinZip in 2006.`,facts:[`PKZIP and the ZIP format were released in 1989.`,`Stac won $120 million from Microsoft in 1994.`]},"market:longdist":{intro:`Telephone calls between cities and countries, carried over trunk cables, microwave towers and undersea links. For decades it is the most profitable wire in the world: priced far above its cost, its earnings help pay for cheap local phone service.`,how:`The phone company sets a price per minute by distance and time of day, approved by the government. Calls share trunk lines, so each extra call costs very little, and profits are high while one firm holds the monopoly. Rivals must build their own long lines or lease them, and each new technology cuts the cost of carrying a minute.`,history:`In 1951 Americans could first dial long-distance calls themselves, starting in Englewood, New Jersey. TAT-1, the first transatlantic telephone cable, opened in 1956 with 36 circuits. In 1969 MCI won permission to sell private lines between Chicago and St Louis. The breakup of AT&T in 1984 opened long distance to competition across America. WorldCom, which had bought MCI, collapsed in an accounting fraud in 2002. Skype, launched in 2003, made calls over the internet free.`,facts:[`TAT-1 carried 36 calls at once in 1956.`,`AT&T was split into seven regional companies and a long-distance firm in 1984.`]},"market:mobile":{intro:`The business of running mobile phone networks: radio towers, licences for slices of the airwaves, and the switches that hand a call from one cell to the next as a car drives along. Subscribers pay a monthly bill and by the minute, and the network belongs to the operator.`,how:`Governments license radio spectrum to a few operators in each country, so competition is limited and profits can be large. Towers are costly to build, but once a network covers the country each extra customer is cheap to add. Operators compete on coverage, price and discounted phones, and earn more as people move from calls to texts and data.`,history:`NTT opened the first cellular network, in Tokyo, in 1979. Ameritech opened America's first, in Chicago, in 1983. Vodafone carried Britain's first mobile call on 1 January 1985. Finland's Radiolinja opened the first digital GSM network in 1991. In 2000 Vodafone bought Germany's Mannesmann for about $180 billion, then the largest takeover in history. World subscriptions reached about 6.3 billion in 2012.`,facts:[`There were about 11 million mobile subscriptions in the world in 1990.`,`Britain's 3G spectrum auction raised £22.5 billion in 2000.`]},"market:satcom":{intro:`Telephone circuits and television feeds sent through communications satellites parked over the oceans, which link continents without laying cables. The customers are phone companies and broadcasters, renting circuits by the year, or by the hour for live events.`,how:`A satellite 36,000 km above the equator circles at the Earth's own pace, so it stays over one spot, dishes can point at it permanently, and it can see a third of the planet. Operators lease circuits or whole transponders, the satellite's radio channels. Satellites cost hundreds of millions to build and launch, while each cable can carry more on busy routes, so satellites win on thin routes, remote places and live TV.`,history:`Intelsat's Early Bird, launched in 1965, carried 240 telephone circuits across the Atlantic. Pictures of the Moon landing reached much of the world through Intelsat satellites in 1969. Inmarsat was founded in 1979 to serve ships at sea. TAT-8, the first fibre-optic cable across the Atlantic, opened in 1988 and took over the busiest routes. PanAmSat launched its first satellite in 1988, the first private operator to compete with Intelsat. Intelsat itself was privatised in 2001.`,facts:[`Early Bird carried 240 phone circuits in 1965.`,`TAT-8 carried about 40,000 calls at once when it opened in 1988.`]},"market:timeshare":{intro:`Renting computer power instead of owning a computer: a service bureau runs a client's payroll or accounts on its own machines, and timesharing lets many users at once type at terminals linked by phone line to one big computer. Customers pay by the hour connected and by the processor seconds they use.`,how:`One big computer is shared among dozens of users, switching between them so fast that each feels it is theirs alone. Customers pay only for what they use, so small firms, engineers and schools get computing they could never afford to buy. The business lasts as long as renting is cheaper than owning a computer of one's own.`,history:`IBM set up its Service Bureau Corporation in 1957. Dartmouth's timesharing system and its new BASIC language opened to students in 1964. General Electric began selling timesharing based on the Dartmouth system in 1965. Tymshare was founded in California in 1966 and built the Tymnet network. In 1968 Lakeside School in Seattle rented time on a timesharing computer, giving Bill Gates and Paul Allen their first computing. McDonnell Douglas bought Tymshare in 1984, as cheap computers of their own lured customers away.`,facts:[`Lakeside School's Mothers' Club paid for its terminal with a rummage sale in 1968.`,`Dartmouth's system of 1964 let students run BASIC programs from teletypes.`]},"market:modems":{intro:`Modems let computers talk over telephone lines by turning data into tones and back again, at first a few hundred bits a second. Businesses use them to link terminals to distant computers. At first only the phone company's own equipment may be plugged into its lines.`,how:`Each modem must understand the one at the other end, so standards decide everything: makers race to build faster ones, then agree on a common standard and compete on price. Buyers want speed, reliability and compatibility with the most other modems. Prices fall quickly after each standard is settled, and each faster one sends users back to buy again.`,history:`AT&T's Bell 103 data set of 1962 sent 300 bits a second. The Carterfone decision of 1968 let Americans attach their own equipment to the phone network. Hayes's Smartmodem of 1981 could be controlled by software commands, which became the standard everyone copied. The V.34 standard of 1994 reached 28,800 bits a second. 56k modems arrived in 1997 amid a format war between US Robotics and Rockwell, settled by the V.90 standard of 1998. By 2008 broadband had replaced dial-up for most homes in rich countries.`,facts:[`The Bell 103 sent 300 bits a second in 1962.`,`A 56k modem cost about $200 in 1997.`]},"market:netgear":{intro:`The equipment that joins computers into networks: adapter cards that plug each computer into a shared cable, and boxes that link one network to another and pass messages between them. Universities, laboratories and offices buy them, along with the cables and the training for the engineers who run them.`,how:`Products must follow shared standards such as Ethernet, so they compete on speed, reliability and price. The maker whose equipment and commands network engineers learn first has an edge: they recommend what they know, and training courses tie them in. Margins are high on the big boxes at the heart of large networks and thin on cheap office gear.`,history:`DEC, Intel and Xerox published the Ethernet standard in 1980. 3Com, co-founded by Ethernet's inventor Bob Metcalfe, sold Ethernet cards for IBM PCs from 1982. Stanford staff Len Bosack and Sandy Lerner founded Cisco in 1984 to sell routers. Juniper launched its first router in 1998 and challenged Cisco at the core of the internet. Cisco briefly became the world's most valuable company in March 2000, worth over $500 billion. By 2012 China's Huawei had become a strong rival in network gear.`,facts:[`Cisco was worth over $500 billion in March 2000.`,`Cisco's revenue was about $40 billion in its 2010 fiscal year.`]},"market:carriergear":{intro:`The equipment telephone networks are built from: exchanges that connect calls, cables, microwave towers, undersea repeaters and radio gear. The buyers are the phone companies, a few dozen in each country and often owned by the state, and every new network they build is an order worth millions.`,how:`Contracts are huge and long, and the equipment must work for decades without failing, so phone companies buy from trusted suppliers, often a national champion. Each shift to a new technology, such as electronic or digital exchanges, reshuffles the suppliers. Spending swings with the phone companies' building plans, booming when they all build at once and crashing when they stop.`,history:`In 1947 Western Electric was the sole supplier of equipment for America's Bell telephone network. AT&T opened the first electronic telephone exchange, at Succasunna, New Jersey, in 1965. Sweden's Ericsson launched its AXE digital exchange in 1976. The fibre boom drove spending to a peak in 2000, and the bust of 2002 halved it. Nortel, once worth about $250 billion, went bankrupt in 2009. Huawei passed Ericsson as the biggest telecom equipment maker in 2012.`,facts:[`Nortel was worth about $250 billion at its peak in 2000.`,`Huawei's revenue passed Ericsson's in 2012, at about $35 billion.`]},"market:access":{intro:`Getting people online from home: a modem dials a service's computers over the telephone line, and the customer pays for the time connected. Homes and small businesses are the customers, and the faster and cheaper the link becomes, the more they use it.`,how:`Providers charge by the hour or by the month, and their costs are modems, phone lines, network links and customer support. Flat fees bring heavy use, so capacity must grow faster than revenue. Whoever owns the phone line or the cable to the home has the advantage, and the monthly price matters more than anything else.`,history:`CompuServe opened its service to home computer owners in 1979, charging by the hour. In December 1996 AOL switched to a flat $19.95 a month, and users met busy signals for months. Cable modems and DSL lines brought always-on broadband to homes from 1999. America had more broadband than dial-up homes by 2005. The world had about 650 million broadband lines in 2012.`,facts:[`CompuServe charged about $5 an hour in the early 1980s.`,`AOL's flat rate was $19.95 a month in 1996.`]},"market:hosting":{intro:`Renting space, power and network links for other people's servers: a company puts its machines in a secure building full of air conditioning, backup generators and fibre from many carriers. Small websites rent space on a shared server instead.`,how:`Hosts charge per rack or cage, per kilowatt of power and per megabit of traffic, or a monthly fee for shared hosting. The building is costly to fit out, so the business depends on filling it. Customers choose on reliability, location and how many networks meet inside, since being close to many carriers makes traffic cheap and fast.`,history:`In 1995 the first web hosting firms began renting space on their servers to anyone who wanted a website. Equinix was founded in 1998 to build neutral buildings where many networks could meet. Rackspace, founded in 1998, sold hosting with round-the-clock support. Exodus Communications was worth about $30 billion at its peak in 2000. Exodus went bankrupt in September 2001 as its dot-com customers vanished. Rackspace listed on the stock market in 2008.`,facts:[`Exodus was worth about $30 billion in 2000 and bankrupt in 2001.`,`GoDaddy ran its first Super Bowl advert in 2005.`]},"market:iaas":{intro:`Virtual servers rented by the hour over the internet: anyone with a credit card can start a hundred machines in minutes and switch them off when done. Startups, websites and researchers use them instead of buying hardware.`,how:`The provider runs huge data centres and slices servers into virtual machines, billing by the hour, the gigabyte and the request. Customers pay more per hour in exchange for no upfront cost and no waiting. The economics favour the biggest providers, who buy hardware and power cheapest and keep machines busiest, and prices keep falling as they compete.`,history:`Amazon launched S3 storage in March 2006 and EC2 servers in August 2006, at 10 cents an hour. Rackspace launched its cloud servers in 2008. Microsoft opened Windows Azure in 2010. NASA and Rackspace launched OpenStack, free software for building clouds, in 2010. Google opened Compute Engine in 2012. In 2013 the CIA chose Amazon over IBM for a $600 million cloud contract.`,facts:[`A small EC2 server cost 10 cents an hour in 2006.`,`Amazon's cloud earned $4.6 billion in 2014, the first year Amazon reported it.`]},"market:bureau":{intro:`Companies too small to own a computer send their records to a bureau that processes them on its own machines: payroll, invoices, stock lists and accounts, run overnight and returned as printed reports and cheques. Clients pay per pay slip or per record.`,how:`The bureau buys a big computer and keeps it busy with many clients' work, so its cost per job is far below what any client could manage. Payroll must be right every week and follow every tax rule, so clients who trust a bureau seldom move. Growth comes from salespeople signing up small firms one by one.`,history:`Henry Taub founded Automatic Payrolls, later ADP, in New Jersey in 1949, doing payroll by hand and with machines. J Lyons and Co's LEO computer ran the world's first routine office job, valuing bakery output, in 1951. In the 1950s Lyons also ran payroll for other companies on LEO. ADP listed on the stock market in 1961 and bought its first computer around then. Paychex, founded in 1971, served the smallest firms. By 1981 ADP paid about 5% of American workers.`,facts:[`LEO ran the world's first routine office computer job in 1951.`,`ADP handled the pay of about one American worker in six in the 2010s.`]},"market:erp":{intro:`Software that helps run a company from one shared database: planning what the factories must make and which parts to buy, then stock, purchasing, accounts and orders. Manufacturers are the first buyers, since one missing part can stop a whole production line.`,how:`Vendors sell large licences plus yearly maintenance, and consultants earn several times more installing them, since each company's ways of working must be set up in the system. Projects take months or years and sometimes fail badly. Once installed, the system holds the company's processes and data, so customers stay for decades and pay maintenance every year.`,history:`Five former IBM engineers founded SAP in Germany in 1972. SAP's R/2 for mainframes followed in 1979. Gartner coined the term ERP, enterprise resource planning, around 1990. SAP's R/3, for networks of smaller servers, arrived in 1992 and made it a giant. In 1999 Hershey's troubled new system left it unable to ship sweets in time for Halloween. Oracle bought PeopleSoft for $10.3 billion in 2005 after a hostile bid.`,facts:[`Hershey's 1999 system problems cost it about $100 million of orders.`,`Oracle paid $10.3 billion for PeopleSoft in 2005.`,`SAP's revenue was €16.2 billion in 2012.`]},"market:crm":{intro:`Customer relationship management software keeps every customer, contact, call and deal in one shared database, so a sales team can see whom to call next and managers can forecast sales. Call centres and marketing departments use it too.`,how:`Vendors sell licences per user to sales departments, with consulting to set it up. Managers buy it for the view it gives them; salespeople resent typing in their notes, so ease of use decides whether the system gets used at all. The leader's data and workflows become part of how a company sells, which makes switching hard.`,history:`Tom Siebel, once a star salesman at Oracle, founded Siebel Systems in 1993 to sell software for sales teams. Salesforce launched its sales software in the browser, rented by the month, in 2000. By 2002 Siebel held about 45% of the CRM market. Microsoft launched its own CRM in 2003. Oracle bought Siebel for $5.85 billion in 2006. By 2012 Salesforce had become the largest CRM vendor, renting its software to over 100,000 companies.`,facts:[`Siebel held about 45% of the CRM market in 2002.`,`Oracle paid $5.85 billion for Siebel in 2006.`]},"market:office":{intro:`The machines and software that offices use for writing, sums and presentations. The first are electric typewriters with a memory, which store a letter on tape so a typist can correct it and print it again without retyping. Secretaries, clerks, lawyers and managers are the users, and every office is a customer.`,how:`Products sell on features and on the files everyone already exchanges: a firm whose documents are in one format sticks with the program that opens them. Staff resist learning a new tool, so a familiar one keeps its users. Vendors bundle several programs into suites priced below the separate parts, and a bundle on every new computer is hard to beat.`,history:`IBM's MT/ST of 1964 recorded typing on magnetic tape, so letters could be corrected and retyped automatically. Wang's word-processing systems filled offices from 1976. VisiCalc, the first spreadsheet, made people buy Apple IIs from 1979. Lotus 1-2-3 made the IBM PC a business machine in 1983. Microsoft bundled Word, Excel and PowerPoint as Office in 1990. Google Docs, free in the browser, launched in 2006.`,facts:[`VisiCalc cost $100 in 1979.`,`Lotus 1-2-3 sold about $53 million in its first year, 1983.`]},"market:cloudapps":{intro:`Business software rented by the user each month and used in a web browser, run on the vendor's servers instead of the customer's. A small team can start in a day with a credit card, with nothing to install and updates arriving by themselves.`,how:`Customers pay subscriptions, so revenue arrives slowly at first, but each year's customers keep paying and the total piles up. The vendor runs one copy of the software for all its customers, keeping costs down and updates fast. Sales and marketing eat most of the early money; the profit comes from customers who stay for years.`,history:`Marc Benioff founded Salesforce in a San Francisco flat in 1999. NetSuite, backed by Larry Ellison, began selling accounting online the same year, 1999. Salesforce listed on the stock market in 2004. Workday was founded in 2005 by PeopleSoft's founder Dave Duffield and Aneel Bhusri after Oracle took over PeopleSoft. Microsoft launched Office 365 in 2011. SAP bought SuccessFactors for $3.4 billion in 2012.`,facts:[`Salesforce's revenue passed $1 billion in its 2009 fiscal year.`,`SAP paid $3.4 billion for SuccessFactors in 2012.`]},"market:online":{intro:`Dial-up services that offer everything inside one walled garden: mail, chat rooms, news, forums, shopping and games, reached by modem from a home computer. Members pay by the hour and see only what the service offers.`,how:`The service owns the content and the network and charges members for connect time, so long chats are profitable. Partners pay to be inside the garden, and marketing with free trial disks costs a fortune. Members join for the friends and forums already there, and leave if something richer and cheaper appears.`,history:`CompuServe and The Source both opened to home users in 1979. Prodigy, from IBM and Sears, went national in 1990 with a flat fee and adverts on screen. In 1993 AOL began mailing free trial disks to millions of American homes. In 1996 AOL switched to a flat monthly fee. AOL took over CompuServe's members in 1998. In 2006 AOL made its service free to anyone with broadband.`,facts:[`CompuServe had over 3 million members in 1995.`,`AOL had over 20 million members in 2000.`]},"market:webads":{intro:`Banner adverts on popular web pages, sold by the thousand times they are shown. Portals, sites that gather news, mail, search and shopping in one place, draw the biggest audiences and sell the most banners.`,how:`Advertisers pay per thousand views, so traffic is everything: portals buy any site with an audience and fight to be the page a browser opens first. Ad networks place adverts across thousands of sites and track who saw what with small files called cookies. Few people click, so prices depend on how much advertisers believe banners work.`,history:`HotWired ran the first banner adverts, one of them from AT&T, in October 1994. Yahoo was founded by two Stanford students in 1994 and became the leading portal. DoubleClick, founded in 1996, served adverts across many sites. In 1999 Yahoo bought GeoCities for $3.6 billion and Broadcast.com for $5.7 billion. Online advert prices fell sharply after the crash of 2000. In 2008 Google bought DoubleClick for $3.1 billion, and Microsoft offered $44.6 billion for Yahoo, which turned it down.`,facts:[`AT&T's banner of 1994 was clicked by about 44% of those who saw it.`,`Yahoo was worth about $125 billion at its peak in 2000.`]},"market:searchads":{intro:`Adverts shown beside search results and matched to the words the searcher typed. The advertiser pays only when someone clicks, and only to reach people who are already looking for what it sells, from plumbers and florists to airlines and banks.`,how:`Advertisers bid on keywords in an auction, and the search engine ranks adverts by bid and by how likely each is to be clicked, so relevant adverts cost less. The engine with the most searches has the most valuable auctions, which pays for a better engine, which brings more searches. Costs are low and margins very high.`,history:`Bill Gross's GoTo.com began selling places in search results by auction, paid per click, in 1998. Google launched AdWords in 2000 and moved it to a pay-per-click auction in 2002. Yahoo bought Overture, as GoTo had been renamed, for $1.63 billion in 2003. Google listed on the stock market in 2004, with search adverts paying for nearly everything it did. Microsoft launched Bing in 2009 and took over Yahoo's search in 2010. Google's advertising revenue reached about $43.7 billion in 2012.`,facts:[`Yahoo paid $1.63 billion for Overture in 2003.`,`Google earned about $43.7 billion from adverts in 2012.`]},"market:ecommerce":{intro:`Shops with no shops: websites where people browse an endless catalogue, pay by card and wait for a parcel. Books, music and computers suit it best, because buyers know exactly what they are getting without touching it, and a website can list far more than any high street shop.`,how:`An online shop can list millions of items with no shelves, but every order must be picked, packed and posted, so margins are thin. The winners invest in warehouses, delivery and fast, cheap shipping, and in reviews and recommendations that make choosing easy. Marketplaces let other sellers list on the site and take a fee without holding any stock.`,history:`Amazon sold its first book in July 1995, and eBay began as AuctionWeb in September 1995. Amazon listed on the stock market in 1997. Pets.com and many other online shops collapsed in 2000. Alibaba launched Taobao in 2003 to fight eBay in China. Amazon Prime promised two-day shipping for $79 a year in 2005. Online sales were about one dollar in twenty of American retail sales in 2012.`,facts:[`Online shopping was 0.7% of US retail sales in 1999.`,`Amazon Prime cost $79 a year in 2005.`]},"market:payments":{intro:`Services that move money online: they let a buyer pay a website, or a friend, with a card or a stored balance, and pass the money on to the seller. They take a small percentage of every payment.`,how:`The fee is a few per cent of each payment, so volume is everything, and much of the fee goes on to banks and card networks. The real enemy is fraud: stolen cards and fake sellers can wipe out a careless service's profits. Services that sit inside a big marketplace, or that developers can add with a few lines of code, grow fastest.`,history:`Confinity launched PayPal in 1999, letting people send money by e-mail. PayPal merged with Elon Musk's X.com in 2000. eBay bought PayPal for $1.5 billion in 2002. Alibaba launched Alipay in 2004, holding a buyer's money until the goods arrived. Adyen was founded in Amsterdam in 2006. Stripe, founded by the Irish brothers Patrick and John Collison, launched in 2011. PayPal moved about $145 billion in 2012.`,facts:[`eBay paid $1.5 billion for PayPal in 2002.`,`PayPal processed about $145 billion of payments in 2012.`]},"market:social":{intro:`Websites where people make a profile, link to their friends and see what those friends post and share. Old classmates, distant relatives and new friends end up in one list. The sites are free to join and earn from advertising aimed at what members reveal about themselves.`,how:`Each new member makes the network more useful to their friends, so the biggest grows fastest. Costs are servers and engineers; revenue comes from adverts that can target age, place and interests. But a network that slows down or fills with spam can empty quickly, as members follow their friends to the next one.`,history:`SixDegrees.com launched in 1997, the first site to combine profiles and friend lists. Friendster launched in 2002 and buckled under its own traffic. MySpace launched in 2003, and Mark Zuckerberg launched Facebook at Harvard in 2004. News Corp bought MySpace for $580 million in 2005. Facebook passed MySpace in visitors worldwide in 2008. News Corp sold MySpace for $35 million in 2011.`,facts:[`News Corp paid $580 million for MySpace in 2005.`,`News Corp sold MySpace for $35 million in 2011.`,`Facebook had 1 billion monthly users in 2012.`]},"market:webvideo":{intro:`Films and video rented or watched through the internet: DVDs chosen on a website and mailed to the door, and clips played in the browser, small and jerky over dial-up. Households pay by the rental or by the month.`,how:`Rental by post charges a monthly fee for as many discs as a member can send back. Video sites pay for vast amounts of storage and bandwidth and earn from adverts. Streaming needs licences from film studios, who raise their prices as a service grows, so content and bandwidth eat half the money, and the winners build huge subscriber bases or make their own shows.`,history:`Netflix began renting DVDs by post in 1998. Netflix switched to a monthly subscription with no late fees in 1999. YouTube was founded in 2005, and Google bought it for $1.65 billion in 2006. Netflix began streaming films to computers in 2007. Hulu launched in 2008. In 2011 Netflix raised prices and tried to split off its DVDs as Qwikster, losing about 800,000 subscribers.`,facts:[`Blockbuster declined to buy Netflix for $50 million in 2000.`,`Google paid $1.65 billion for YouTube in 2006.`]},"market:apps":{intro:`Programs for smartphones, bought for a dollar or two or free with adverts, and sold through the single store each phone platform runs. Games, maps, photo tools, messengers and social apps fill the stores, written by everyone from giant firms to teenagers in their bedrooms.`,how:`The platform owner takes 30% of each sale and decides what may be sold, so developers live by its rules. Hundreds of thousands of apps compete for the top charts, which bring most of the downloads, so a hit can make a small team rich overnight. Prices fall towards free, and money moves to adverts and purchases inside the app.`,history:`Apple opened the App Store in July 2008 with about 500 apps, and Google's Android Market followed in October 2008. In 2009 Apple let free apps sell extras inside them. Rovio's Angry Birds arrived in 2009 and sold millions of copies at 99 cents. Apple's store passed 10 billion downloads in January 2011. Facebook paid $1 billion for Instagram, a 13-person app maker, in 2012. Google renamed its store Google Play in 2012.`,facts:[`The App Store opened with about 500 apps in 2008.`,`Apple had paid developers $5 billion by 2012.`]},"market:arcade":{intro:`Coin-operated video games in cabinets, placed in bars, arcades, shopping centres and cinemas. Players pay a coin a go and compete for the high score, and the owner of the machine shares the takings with the place that hosts it.`,how:`Makers sell cabinets to operators, who buy whichever games earn the most coins per week and swap them as earnings fall. Games are designed to be learned in seconds and to end quickly, so players keep feeding coins. A hit sells tens of thousands of cabinets, and earnings drop as a craze fades or home machines catch up.`,history:`Nutting Associates sold Computer Space, the first coin-operated video game, in 1971, and Atari's Pong of 1972 started the boom. Taito's Space Invaders of 1978 became a sensation in Japan and around the world. Namco's Pac-Man of 1980 became the best-selling arcade game of all, with about 400,000 cabinets. North American arcades took over $5 billion in 1981. By 1984 the craze had faded and arcades closed across America. Capcom's Street Fighter II brought players back in 1991.`,facts:[`Pac-Man sold about 400,000 cabinets from 1980.`,`North American arcades took about $2.8 billion in 1980.`]},"market:console":{intro:`Game machines that plug into the family television: the box is sold cheaply, sometimes at a loss, and the games are sold dear. Whoever makes the box can earn from every game played on it.`,how:`Console makers often lose money on the hardware and earn a licence fee on each cartridge or disc, controlling who may publish. Players buy the machine with the games they want, and publishers make games for the machine with the most players, so each generation becomes a war fought with exclusive hits. Each new, more powerful generation restarts the race, and the leader can change.`,history:`Magnavox's Odyssey, designed by Ralph Baer, went on sale in 1972. Atari's VCS of 1977, with swappable cartridges, became the hit of its era. In 1983 a flood of poor games crashed the American market. Nintendo's NES revived the business in America in 1985. Sony's PlayStation of 1994 moved games onto cheap CDs. Nintendo's Wii of 2006 won new players with motion controls.`,facts:[`US console sales fell from about $3.2 billion in 1982 to about $100 million in 1985.`,`The PlayStation 2 of 2000 sold more than 150 million units.`,`The Wii sold about 101 million units after its 2006 launch.`]},"market:handheld":{intro:`Portable electronic games that run on batteries and fit in a hand: a little display of glowing red lights, a few buttons and a game built in. Children, commuters and travellers buy them to play anywhere, away from the television and the arcade.`,how:`The winners are cheap, tough and long-lasting on batteries rather than the most powerful: battery life and price beat colour and speed. A maker with famous characters sells the machine through its own games, and earns licence fees from other publishers too. Machines are often bought as gifts, so the Christmas season makes or breaks a year.`,history:`Mattel's handheld Football, a game of red LED blips, was a hit in 1977. Milton Bradley's Microvision of 1979 was the first handheld with swappable cartridges. Nintendo's Game & Watch, created by Gunpei Yokoi, launched in 1980. Nintendo's Game Boy of 1989 came with Tetris, and beat Sega's colour Game Gear of 1990, which ate batteries. Pokémon on the Game Boy started a worldwide craze in 1996. Nintendo's DS and Sony's PSP arrived in 2004.`,facts:[`Game & Watch sold about 43 million units from 1980.`,`The Game Boy and Game Boy Color sold about 119 million together (1989 to 2003).`,`The Nintendo DS sold about 154 million after its 2004 launch.`]},"market:pcgames":{intro:`Games for home and personal computers, sold on cassette tapes and floppy disks in plastic bags and boxes. Text adventures, strategy games and simulations thrive here, since computer owners have keyboards and patience. The players are mostly teenagers and hobbyists, many of whom learn to program by taking games apart.`,how:`Anyone with a computer can write and sell a game, so small studios and bedroom programmers prosper. Publishers sell through shops, with big boxes and thick manuals; copying a disk is easy, so piracy eats sales and makers try copy protection. The newest games push players to upgrade their machines, tying game sales to the hardware trade.`,history:`Scott Adams's Adventureland, sold on cassette in 1978, was the first commercial adventure game for home computers. Electronic Arts was founded in 1982 and promoted its game designers like rock stars. Sid Meier's Civilization appeared in 1991. Myst and id Software's shareware Doom both came out in 1993, and Doom made the PC the home of shooters. Valve's Steam began selling games as downloads in 2003. World sales of PC games in shops reached about $5.9 billion in 2007.`,facts:[`The Sims overtook Myst as the best-selling PC game ever in 2002.`,`Myst sold over 6 million copies after its 1993 release.`]},"market:onlinegames":{intro:`Games whose worlds and matches live on the publisher's servers, played with thousands of others over the internet. Massively multiplayer online games keep a world running even when a player logs off, and charge a monthly subscription.`,how:`Players pay by the month, or play free and buy items, and the publisher must run servers, support and new content for years. Friends and guilds keep players in a world, so a hit can earn for a decade. In Korea, internet cafés called PC rooms rent computers by the hour and drive the market.`,history:`Ultima Online of 1997 was the first online world to draw a mass audience. NCsoft's Lineage, launched in Korea in 1998, filled the country's new PC rooms. EverQuest launched in 1999, the same year Korea's Nexon began giving games away and selling virtual items. Blizzard's World of Warcraft launched in 2004. Riot Games released League of Legends free in 2009, and Tencent bought most of Riot in 2011.`,facts:[`World of Warcraft had about 12 million subscribers in 2010.`,`Tencent bought most of Riot Games for about $400 million in 2011.`]},"market:mobilegames":{intro:`Games played on mobile phones: simple games built into the handset, and small games bought from the phone network's menu for a few dollars each. Small screens and keypads favour quick, simple games that fill a bus ride or a queue.`,how:`Games are sold through the phone networks, which keep a large cut and decide what goes on their menus, and each game must be rebuilt for hundreds of handset models. The money per player is small, but the audience is anyone with a phone. Big publishers buy the licences of famous brands to stand out on a cramped menu.`,history:`Nokia put Snake on its 6110 phone in 1997. Nokia's N-Gage game phone of 2003 flopped. Electronic Arts bought JAMDAT, a leading mobile game publisher, for $680 million in 2005. Apple's App Store let any developer sell games straight to players from 2008. Rovio's Angry Birds passed a billion downloads in 2012. King's Candy Crush Saga reached phones in 2012, free to download and paid for by the few who bought extra lives.`,facts:[`Electronic Arts paid $680 million for JAMDAT in 2005.`,`Mobile games earned about $12.3 billion in 2013.`]},"market:gpucards":{intro:`Add-in cards with graphics chips that draw 3D worlds for PC games: the more triangles and pixels a card can push each second, the better a game looks and the smoother it runs. Gamers buy them by the million and upgrade every couple of years.`,how:`Chip designers sell graphics processors to card makers, who add memory and coolers and sell boxed cards in shops. Reviews and speed tests decide sales, and the fastest card at the top sells the cheaper ones below it. Drivers and game deals matter too: a big new game that runs best on one maker's cards sells them.`,history:`3dfx's Voodoo Graphics card of 1996 made fast 3D affordable for PC gamers. Voodoo2 cards of 1998 could be paired in one PC for double speed. ATI's Radeon 9700 of 2002 beat NVIDIA's best, and NVIDIA's noisy GeForce FX of 2003 was mocked. NVIDIA brought back card pairing as SLI in 2004. The GeForce 8800 GTX of 2006 had unified shaders that could also run general programs. In 2012 two GeForce GTX 580 cards trained AlexNet.`,facts:[`A GeForce GTX 580 cost $499 in 2010.`,`NVIDIA's gaming revenue was about $12.5 billion in its 2022 fiscal year.`]},"market:engines":{intro:`Game engines are the reusable core of a game: the code that draws the graphics, plays the sound, simulates physics and loads the levels. Engine makers license them to other studios, who build their own games on top.`,how:`A studio pays a licence fee per game, sometimes with a royalty on sales, rather than spend years writing its own technology. The engine behind famous games draws more studios, whose tools and staff then depend on it. The money is small next to the games themselves, but the influence over how games are made is large.`,history:`id Software licensed its Doom engine to other studios from 1994. Epic's Unreal engine of 1998 became the main rival to id's Quake engines. Criterion's RenderWare powered many PlayStation 2 games, including Grand Theft Auto III in 2001. Electronic Arts bought Criterion in 2004, and many studios left RenderWare. Unity launched at Apple's developer conference in 2005 as a cheap engine for the Mac. In 2009 Unity released a free version and Epic its free Unreal Development Kit.`,facts:[`Unity had over a million registered developers by 2012.`,`Unreal Engine 4 charged a 5% royalty on game revenue from 2015.`]},"market:expertsys":{intro:`Programs that capture a human expert's know-how as hundreds or thousands of if-then rules, then use them to diagnose faults, configure orders or advise on loans. They are sold as shells, empty rule engines to fill in, together with consulting and custom rule bases.`,how:`Specialists called knowledge engineers interview experts and write the rules, which makes every system a costly custom project. Vendors sell development tools, often on special workstations, plus services to big firms and the military. The systems work in narrow fields but break down outside them, and the rules need constant upkeep as the business changes.`,history:`DEC began using XCON, a rule-based system that configured orders for its VAX computers, in 1980. Japan's Fifth Generation project of 1982 set off a race in AI spending. American sales of expert-system tools reached about $74 million in 1985. By 1986 XCON was saving DEC an estimated $25 million a year. From 1987 the market shrank as buyers found how much upkeep the rules needed, a slump later called an AI winter. By 1996 the survivors were selling business rules software.`,facts:[`US sales of expert-system tools were about $20 million in 1984.`,`XCON saved DEC an estimated $25 million a year by 1986.`]},"market:lispmach":{intro:`Workstations built to run Lisp, the favourite programming language of artificial intelligence research. Their hardware checks data types and tidies up unused memory by itself, so big AI programs run fast and programmers get a rich, windowed environment.`,how:`Makers sell to AI labs, defence contractors and companies building expert systems, at tens of thousands of dollars a machine. The special hardware is fast for Lisp but costly to keep improving, so the machines depend on staying ahead of ordinary workstations. If general-purpose computers become fast enough to run Lisp in software, the special machines lose their reason to exist.`,history:`Richard Greenblatt and Tom Knight built the first Lisp machines at MIT's AI Lab in the 1970s. Two companies, Lisp Machines Inc and Symbolics, were founded around 1980 to sell them. Symbolics.com became the first registered .com domain in 1985. Symbolics' revenue reached $114 million in its 1986 fiscal year. In 1987 the market collapsed as Sun workstations ran the same Lisp for far less. Symbolics filed for bankruptcy in 1993.`,facts:[`Symbolics.com was the first .com domain, registered in 1985.`,`Lisp machine sales were about $229 million in 1984.`]},"market:reading":{intro:`Machines that read printed or written text: page scanners that turn typed documents into computer text, postal readers that sort letters by address, and bank machines that read the amounts on cheques. Banks, post offices and governments pay to cut the cost of typing everything by hand.`,how:`A scanner captures the page and software recognises each character by matching shapes or features. Accuracy is everything: every mistake must be fixed by a person, so buyers pay per item read or per machine according to how much typing it saves. Neat machine print is easy, while handwriting is hard.`,history:`In 1965 the US Post Office began using optical readers to sort mail by address. Ray Kurzweil's Kurzweil Reading Machine of 1976 read books aloud for blind people. Caere's OmniPage of 1988 brought OCR to personal computers. ABBYY released FineReader in 1993. By 1998 NCR's cheque readers, built on Yann LeCun's convolutional neural networks, read more than a tenth of American cheques. Google began scanning millions of library books in 2004.`,facts:[`The Kurzweil Reading Machine cost about $50,000 in 1976.`,`LeCun's networks read over 10% of US cheques in the late 1990s.`]},"market:dictation":{intro:`Software that turns speech into text or commands: doctors and lawyers dictating reports, call centres that let callers speak instead of pressing keys, and phones that dial a name when told. Early systems must be trained to each user's voice and need words spoken one at a time.`,how:`Programs compare the sounds of speech with statistical models of words and of likely word sequences, and improve as they adapt to one speaker. Buyers pay for licences per seat, and specialist versions for medicine and law fetch high prices for their vocabularies. Accuracy, speed and tolerance of noise decide sales; every error a user must correct eats into the benefit.`,history:`Dragon Systems released DragonDictate for PCs in 1990, recognising words spoken one at a time. Dragon NaturallySpeaking of 1997 was the first PC program to handle continuous speech, at $695. IBM answered with ViaVoice in 1997. Lernout & Hauspie bought Dragon in 2000 and collapsed in an accounting fraud the same year. Nuance, rebuilt around ScanSoft in 2005, bought up most of the industry. Apple's Siri, using Nuance's recognition, arrived on the iPhone 4S in 2011.`,facts:[`Dragon NaturallySpeaking cost $695 in 1997.`,`Nuance's revenue was about $389 million in 2006.`]},"market:translation":{intro:`Computers that translate text from one language to another: rough, often word for word, but good enough for an analyst to see what a document is about. Governments, armed forces and international bodies are the main buyers.`,how:`Systems use bilingual dictionaries and grammar rules written by hand, built by linguists over many years for each pair of languages. Buyers pay for licences and custom dictionaries; the results still need human editing, so the value lies in speed and volume. Quality, the number of languages and the subject fields covered decide sales.`,history:`The Georgetown-IBM experiment translated 60 Russian sentences into English in 1954. In 1966 the ALPAC report judged machine translation a failure, and American funding dried up. Systran was founded in 1968 and supplied the US Air Force with Russian translations. The European Commission began using Systran in 1976. AltaVista's free Babel Fish, built on Systran, appeared on the web in 1997. Google launched Translate in 2006, learning from millions of documents already translated by people.`,facts:[`The 1954 Georgetown demonstration used 250 words and six grammar rules.`,`Systran's revenue was about €10.2 million in 2004.`]},"market:chessai":{intro:`Chess-playing computers for the home: boards with a small computer inside that lights up or announces its moves, and chess programs for personal computers. They let a player practise at any hour against an opponent set to a chosen strength.`,how:`Makers sell boards in toy and department stores and programs in software shops, competing on playing strength, published ratings and the feel of the board. Programs search through possible moves, so faster chips mean stronger play. The market is small and loves a headline, since a famous win over a strong player sells more boards than any advert.`,history:`Fidelity Electronics' Chess Challenger, sold in 1977, was the first commercial chess computer. Germany's Hegener + Glaser launched its Mephisto boards in 1980. ChessBase released the program Fritz in 1991. IBM's Deep Blue beat world champion Garry Kasparov in a match in 1997. In 2006 Deep Fritz, running on an ordinary PC, beat world champion Vladimir Kramnik by four games to two. By 2008 free programs played better than any human, and dedicated chess computers had become a niche.`,facts:[`Deep Blue searched about 200 million positions a second in 1997.`,`Deep Fritz beat Kramnik 4 to 2 in 2006.`]},"market:scoring":{intro:`Software that helps banks decide quickly and consistently: credit scores that rate whether a borrower will repay, and fraud models that judge every card payment in a fraction of a second. Card issuers, lenders and insurers pay for it.`,how:`Models learn from millions of past loans and payments which patterns go with bad debts or fraud, and give each case a score. The bank sets a cut-off and passes only the doubtful cases to people. Vendors charge by the account or the transaction; a model that catches more fraud with fewer false alarms saves banks real money, and data pooled from many banks makes models better.`,history:`Fair, Isaac and Company, founded in 1956, launched the general-purpose FICO credit score in 1989. HNC Software's Falcon, built on neural networks, began spotting card fraud in 1992. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac began asking for FICO scores on mortgages in 1995. Fair Isaac bought HNC for about $810 million in 2002. In 2003 a new American law gave everyone the right to see their credit report free once a year. Fair Isaac renamed itself FICO in 2009.`,facts:[`Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac adopted FICO scores for mortgages in 1995.`,`Fair Isaac paid about $810 million for HNC Software in 2002.`]},"market:components":{intro:`Ready-made parts that programmers drop into their own applications: a spreadsheet grid, a chart, a calendar, a report designer. They are sold to developers per seat and shipped royalty-free inside the finished programs, so one clever grid can end up in thousands of business applications.`,how:`Visual programming tools let a developer drag a component onto a form and set its properties, so a ready-made part saves weeks of work. Vendors compete on features, speed and how well they fit the latest tools. When the platform owner gives basic parts away free, vendors move up to richer, specialised ones, and sales follow the popularity of each programming tool.`,history:`Microsoft's Visual Basic of 1991 let developers buy VBX add-ons to drag onto their forms. Trolltech released its Qt toolkit in 1995. Microsoft's ActiveX controls replaced VBX in 1996. Gartner put the components industry at about $500 million in 1997. Microsoft's .NET of 2002 set off a new wave of component vendors. Progress Software bought Telerik, a Bulgarian component maker, for $262.5 million in 2014.`,facts:[`ActiveX component sales were about $240 million in 1996 (Giga).`,`Progress paid $262.5 million for Telerik in 2014.`]},"market:middleware":{intro:`The plumbing between a company's programs: software that passes messages between systems, keeps transactions correct across many databases, and runs business applications on servers. Users never see it, but banks, airlines and retailers cannot run without it.`,how:`Vendors license it per processor at high prices, with yearly support. Once a company has wired its systems together through one product, ripping it out means rewiring everything, so customers stay. Middleware sells on reliability under heavy load, on support for standards and on the skills available; free versions and features bundled into operating systems keep pushing prices down.`,history:`IBM's CICS had handled mainframe transactions since 1969. IBM shipped MQSeries, its messaging software, in 1993. BEA Systems was founded in 1995 and bought the Tuxedo transaction system in 1996. Sun's Java enterprise standard of 1999 made application servers the new battleground, and BEA's WebLogic led it. Oracle bought BEA for $8.5 billion in 2008. Gartner put the market at $19.3 billion in 2011.`,facts:[`Red Hat paid about $350 million for JBoss in 2006.`,`Oracle paid $8.5 billion for BEA in 2008.`,`IBM held about 32% of the market in 2011 (Gartner).`]},"market:designtools":{intro:`The programs designers live in all day: drawing and illustration, photo retouching, page layout and screen design. Graphic designers, photographers, publishers and advertising agencies buy them, usually to run on the Macintosh, and spend years mastering every menu.`,how:`Tools sell by the copy at high prices, with paid upgrades. Professionals learn one program deeply and send files to each other in its format, so a leader keeps its users, and printers and agencies expect the standard tools. Bundles of several programs at one price squeeze rivals that make only one.`,history:`Adobe released Illustrator in January 1987. Photoshop, written by the brothers Thomas and John Knoll, shipped in February 1990. Adobe bought Aldus, maker of FreeHand and PageMaker, in 1994. Adobe launched InDesign in 1999 to challenge QuarkXPress, which held most of the page-layout market. Adobe bought Macromedia for about $3.4 billion in 2005. Sketch, for designing screens, launched in 2010.`,facts:[`Adobe paid about $3.4 billion for Macromedia in 2005.`,`Creative Suite of 2003 sold Photoshop, Illustrator and InDesign together at one price.`]},"market:appbuilders":{intro:`Tools that promise business applications without much programming: fourth-generation languages, report writers and screen-form designers. An analyst describes the screens, data and rules in near-English, and the tool writes the program, far quicker than waiting for the computer department to build it.`,how:`Vendors sell licences per developer and per user, promising applications in weeks instead of months. They sell well to departments with long waiting lists for new systems, but apps that grow complex hit the tool's limits and need real programmers. Each tool ties its apps to its own runtime, so customers either stay or rewrite.`,history:`James Martin's 1981 book Application Development Without Programmers named the dream. Apple's HyperCard of 1987 let ordinary Mac owners build programs from cards and buttons. Powersoft's PowerBuilder of 1991 became a hit for building apps on networks of PCs. Microsoft's Access database of 1992 put app building on office PCs. Sybase bought Powersoft for about $904 million in 1995. Salesforce opened Force.com in 2008 for building apps on its cloud.`,facts:[`Sybase paid about $904 million for Powersoft in 1995.`,`HyperCard came free with every new Macintosh from 1987.`]},"market:musicsw":{intro:`Programs for making music on a computer: sequencers that record and play back notes on synthesizers, and then whole recording studios that capture, edit and mix sound on the screen. Musicians, producers and studios buy them.`,how:`Programs sell by the copy, with paid upgrades, and each competes on its way of working, which musicians learn and seldom abandon. Studio software often needs special sound hardware, which its maker sells too. Other firms sell plug-in instruments and effects that run inside the main programs, so the host with the most plug-ins attracts more users.`,history:`MIDI, the standard that lets synthesizers and computers talk, appeared in 1983. Steinberg's first sequencer, Pro-16, came out for the Commodore 64 in 1984. Digidesign's Pro Tools arrived in 1991 and turned a Macintosh into a recording studio. Steinberg introduced VST plug-ins with Cubase in 1996. Ableton released Live in 2001, built for performing on stage as well as recording. Apple bought Emagic, maker of Logic, in 2002, and stopped selling it for Windows.`,facts:[`The Atari ST of 1985 was the first popular home computer with MIDI built in.`,`Avid bought Digidesign, maker of Pro Tools, in 1995.`]},"building:garage":{intro:`The garage start-up is the founding legend of the technology industry: two or three friends, a borrowed workbench, a phone line and an idea, working where the car should be. The rent is nothing, the hours are endless, and everyone does every job.`,how:`A garage company lives on savings, credit cards and its first customers' orders. The founders build prototypes by hand, answer the phone themselves and hire their friends. It works until real orders or investors arrive: then the team needs desks, meeting rooms and a proper address, and the company moves out.`,history:`In 1939 Bill Hewlett and David Packard founded HP in a rented garage at 367 Addison Avenue, Palo Alto, and built an audio oscillator there. Walt Disney's studio bought eight of those oscillators for the film Fantasia in 1940. Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak assembled early Apple I computers at the Jobs family home in Los Altos in 1976. California named the HP garage the birthplace of Silicon Valley in 1989. Jeff Bezos started Amazon in the garage of a rented house in Bellevue, Washington, in 1994. Larry Page and Sergey Brin rented Susan Wojcicki's garage in Menlo Park for Google in 1998.`,facts:[`Hewlett and Packard started HP with $538 in 1939.`,`Google paid $1,700 a month for its Menlo Park garage in 1998.`]},"building:office":{intro:`An office floor is a rented storey of a city building fitted out for one company: rows of desks, meeting rooms, a kitchen and a cupboard for the network. For a growing company it means a real address, room to hire, and somewhere to bring customers and investors.`,how:`Companies lease floors for several years, paying rent by the square metre or foot, and spend heavily on wiring, furniture and glass meeting rooms. The layout shapes how people work: open plans are cheap and sociable but noisy, while private offices are quiet but costly. Tech firms add free food and games to win staff when engineers are scarce.`,history:`Frank Lloyd Wright's Larkin Administration Building in Buffalo, finished in 1906, was an early open office built around a central light court. In the 1950s the German Quickborner team designed the Bürolandschaft, an office landscape of desks grouped by how work flowed. Herman Miller's Action Office II of 1968, designed by Robert Propst, gave rise to the cubicle. Google's free canteens, begun in 1999, made free food a Silicon Valley habit. WeWork, founded in 2010, leased whole floors and rented desks out by the month. Its stock market listing collapsed in 2019.`,facts:[`WeWork was valued at $47 billion in early 2019.`,`Robert Propst's Action Office II went on sale in 1968.`,`WeWork filed for bankruptcy in 2023.`]},"building:campus":{intro:`A corporate campus is a company's own grounds: several buildings among lawns, car parks and footpaths, laid out like a university so that researchers and engineers can think, meet and walk. It gathers thousands of staff in one place and tells the world the company has arrived.`,how:`A company buys or leases land and buildings, often in the suburbs where space is cheap, and runs them like a small town: canteens, gyms, shuttle buses, security and maintenance crews. A campus helps to recruit and keep staff and makes chance meetings likelier, but it is costly, ties the company to one place and lengthens commutes.`,history:`Bell Labs moved its researchers to a campus at Murray Hill, New Jersey, in 1941. General Motors opened its Technical Center in Warren, Michigan, designed by Eero Saarinen, in 1956. IBM's Thomas J Watson Research Center, also by Saarinen, opened at Yorktown Heights in 1961. Microsoft moved to its campus in Redmond, Washington, in 1986. Google leased Silicon Graphics' old campus in Mountain View as the Googleplex in 2003, and bought it in 2006. Facebook moved into Sun Microsystems' old campus in Menlo Park in 2011.`,facts:[`Saarinen's Bell Labs building at Holmdel opened in 1962.`,`Google paid $319 million for the Googleplex in 2006.`]},"building:ring":{intro:`A headquarters built as a single statement: one vast ring-shaped building set in parkland, designed to hold a whole company's engineers under one roof. It is part office, part monument, built to last a century and to be photographed from the air.`,how:`Buildings like this take years to design and billions to build, with custom glass, stone, doors and furniture. Everything inside is planned to make people meet: long corridors, shared cafés, a garden in the middle. Critics say they cut a company off from the city around it and lengthen commutes; supporters say they shape its culture for decades.`,history:`Steve Jobs presented his plan for a ring-shaped Apple campus to the Cupertino city council in June 2011, months before he died. Cupertino approved the design by Norman Foster's firm in 2013. Apple Park opened to staff in April 2017, on the site of an old Hewlett-Packard campus. Its ring, about 1.6 km round, used the largest panels of curved glass made up to 2017. Amazon opened its Spheres, three glass domes full of plants, in Seattle in 2018. Google opened its Bay View campus, with tent-like solar roofs, in 2022.`,facts:[`Apple Park reportedly cost about $5 billion (2017).`,`Apple Park was built for about 12,000 staff (2017).`]},"building:mainframeroom":{intro:`The first computer rooms were built around a single machine: a room-sized computer full of vacuum tubes, with its own power supply, air conditioning and a floor strong enough to hold it. Operators fed it punched cards and magnetic tape, and engineers stood by to replace tubes that failed.`,how:`The tubes give off enormous heat, so cooling matters as much as the computer, and a power cut or a hot day can stop the work. A staff of operators, programmers and engineers keeps the machine running around the clock, since it is too expensive to sit idle. Spare hours are sold to other customers.`,history:`ENIAC, unveiled at the University of Pennsylvania in 1946, filled a room with 40 cabinets and 17,468 vacuum tubes and drew about 150 kilowatts. J Lyons and Co's LEO began running office work in its London computer room in 1951. IBM's 701 of 1952 was rented to customers for about $15,000 a month. The SAGE air-defence computers of 1958 each filled a floor of a windowless concrete blockhouse and drew up to 3 megawatts. IBM's small 1401 of 1959 brought computer rooms to thousands of ordinary businesses.`,facts:[`ENIAC used 17,468 vacuum tubes in 1946.`,`Each SAGE computer of 1958 weighed about 250 tonnes.`]},"building:miniroom":{intro:`A minicomputer room is a corner of a laboratory or office given over to a computer the size of one or two refrigerators. It needs ordinary power and modest cooling, and the scientists and engineers who use it can sit at its terminal and run it themselves.`,how:`One machine serves a lab or department, often wired straight to instruments, machine tools or a handful of terminals. Users write their own programs and fix small faults themselves, with a service contract for the rest. Because it fits one department's budget, many departments end up with one of their own, beyond the control of the central computer staff.`,history:`DEC's PDP-1 of 1960 cost about $120,000 and fitted in a few cabinets. MIT students wrote Spacewar!, one of the first video games, on a PDP-1 in 1962. DEC's PDP-8 of 1965 cost $18,000 and was small enough to sit on a lab bench. Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie began Unix on a spare PDP-7 at Bell Labs in 1969. DEC's VAX-11/780 of 1977 served a whole department from one air-conditioned room. Tracy Kidder's book about Data General's race to build a new minicomputer, The Soul of a New Machine, won a Pulitzer Prize in 1982.`,facts:[`A PDP-1 cost about $120,000 in 1960.`,`DEC sold about 50,000 PDP-8s after its 1965 launch.`]},"building:bureau":{intro:`A timesharing bureau is a computer centre that sells time on its machines to customers who dial in from far away. It holds a few big computers, racks of modems and leased telephone lines, and its customers type at teleprinters in their own offices and schools.`,how:`The operating system switches between dozens of users many times a second, so each gets a share of the machine. Customers pay for connect time, processor seconds and storage, and the bureau must keep its machines up day and night. The costs are the computers, the phone lines and the support staff; profit depends on keeping the computers busy without making users wait.`,history:`Fernando Corbató's CTSS at MIT showed timesharing working in 1961. Dartmouth College opened its timesharing system, with the new BASIC language, in 1964. General Electric began selling timesharing based on the Dartmouth design in 1965. Tymshare was founded in California in 1966 and built its own network, Tymnet, to reach customers across America. In 1968 Bill Gates first programmed on a timesharing terminal at Lakeside School in Seattle. CompuServe began in Columbus, Ohio, in 1969, selling spare time on an insurance company's computers.`,facts:[`CTSS first ran on an IBM 709 at MIT in 1961.`,`CompuServe started in 1969 as part of Golden United Life Insurance.`]},"building:glasshouse":{intro:`The glass house is the classic company data centre: a large machine room behind glass walls, where a row of mainframes, disk cabinets and tape drives sits on a raised floor, tended by operators. Visitors and executives can look in on the company's most expensive machines.`,how:`The raised floor hides thick cables and carries chilled air from big air-conditioning units, and some mainframes are cooled by chilled water piped in from a plant room. Shifts of operators mount tapes, load paper and watch consoles around the clock, while schedulers plan the batch jobs that run overnight. Backup power, fire suppression and locked doors guard the company's records.`,history:`IBM announced the System/360 in 1964, and new machine rooms were built for it around the world. American Airlines' SABRE booking system went live in 1964 on two IBM 7090s in a centre at Briarcliff Manor, New York. IBM's 3081 of 1980 needed chilled water to cool its densely packed circuit modules. The Montreal Protocol of 1987 began phasing out the halon gas that had protected computer rooms from fire. By 1995 networks of cheaper servers were emptying many glass houses, though banks and airlines kept their mainframes.`,facts:[`IBM announced six compatible System/360 models at once in 1964.`,`SABRE ran on two IBM 7090 computers in 1964.`]},"building:serverroom":{intro:`A LAN server room is a back room or large cupboard in an office where the local network's servers live: file and print servers, a tape drive for backups, network hubs and a tangle of cables. It is often cooled by an air-conditioning unit added after the machines started overheating.`,how:`One or two administrators keep the servers patched, swap backup tapes each evening and add accounts for new staff. A power cut, a full disk or a failed fan can stop the whole office, so the room fills with battery backup units and spare parts. As a company grows so does the room, until it moves into a proper data centre.`,history:`Bob Metcalfe and colleagues at Xerox PARC invented Ethernet in 1973. Novell's NetWare of 1983 turned a PC into a file server for an office. Compaq's SystemPro of 1989 was among the first PCs built to be a server. Ethernet over cheap telephone-style cable was standardised as 10BASE-T in 1990, and offices were rewired for it. In 1999 administrators combed their server rooms for the Year 2000 date bug. From 2006 rented cloud services began to empty office server rooms.`,facts:[`PARC's first Ethernet of 1973 ran at 2.94 megabits a second.`,`10BASE-T Ethernet ran at 10 megabits a second in 1990.`]},"building:idc":{intro:`An internet data centre is a building made to house servers for many customers, with fibre from many telecom carriers, backup diesel generators, battery rooms and round-the-clock security. Websites, online shops and internet providers rent space by the rack or by the locked cage.`,how:`The building's value lies in its connections: where many networks meet, traffic can pass between them cheaply and fast, so carriers and content firms crowd in together. The operator sells space, power and cross-connects, the cables between customers' cages. Building one costs hundreds of millions, so filling it quickly decides whether it pays.`,history:`MAE-East, one of the first places where internet networks met to swap traffic, opened in the Washington area in 1992. Exodus Communications built data centres for websites across America in the late 1990s. Equinix was founded in 1998 to build neutral buildings that any network could join. Exodus went bankrupt in 2001 as its dot-com customers failed. By 2012 Loudoun County in Northern Virginia, nicknamed Data Center Alley, had become the world's biggest cluster of data centres.`,facts:[`Equinix opened its first data centre, in Ashburn, Virginia, in 1999.`,`Exodus was worth about $30 billion at its peak in 2000.`]},"building:warehouse":{intro:`A warehouse-scale data centre is a plain, enormous building full of identical cheap servers that work together as one giant computer, running a search engine, a social network or a cloud. It is built where power is cheap and the climate cool, often near a hydroelectric dam.`,how:`Instead of buying reliable, expensive machines, the owner buys thousands of simple ones and writes software that carries on when some fail, as some do every day. Most of the running cost is power and cooling, so engineers obsess over efficiency: the total power used divided by the power that reaches the computers. Custom servers, outside air for cooling and careful siting cut the bill.`,history:`Google opened its data centre at The Dalles, Oregon, beside the Columbia River, in 2006. Microsoft opened a giant data centre in Quincy, Washington, near cheap hydropower, in 2007. The Green Grid defined that efficiency measure, power usage effectiveness or PUE, in 2007. Google's Luiz Barroso and Urs Hölzle described the warehouse-scale computer in a book in 2009. Facebook opened its own data centre at Prineville, Oregon, in 2011 and shared its designs as the Open Compute Project.`,facts:[`A typical data centre had a PUE of about 2 in 2007, spending as much on overheads as on computing.`,`Google reported an average PUE of about 1.12 in 2012.`]},"building:closet":{intro:`A server closet is a small rented room packed with a few racks of computers: the first home of many startups' machines, and of research teams that cannot wait for a real data centre. It is loud, hot and cramped, and someone has to be on call when it overheats.`,how:`Power and cooling are the limits: an ordinary building can deliver only so much electricity to one room, and the heat must go somewhere, so portable air conditioners and propped-open doors are common. The owner buys the hardware outright and runs it with a small team. It is cheap to start and quick to set up, but one tripped breaker or failed cooler can stop everything.`,history:`In 1996 Larry Page and Sergey Brin kept Google's first disks in a case built of Lego bricks at Stanford. In 2004 Mark Zuckerberg ran the first Facebook on servers rented for $85 a month. In 2012 Alex Krizhevsky trained AlexNet on two graphics cards in his bedroom at his parents' home in Toronto. From 2013 bitcoin miners filled garages and spare rooms with humming mining machines. By 2023 AI startups were packing GPU servers into any room they could rent with enough power.`,facts:[`Google's Lego disk case of 1996 held ten 4 GB drives.`,`Facebook's first server cost $85 a month in 2004.`]},"building:colo":{intro:`A colocation hall is a big room inside a shared data centre where many companies rent space for their own servers. Customers bring their machines and lock them in cages or rows of racks, while the landlord supplies power, cooling, security and connections to many networks.`,how:`Racks are lined up so their fronts face each other across a cold aisle fed with chilled air, while their hot exhaust blows into a hot aisle where the heat is drawn away. Customers pay per rack and per kilowatt, and for cables to other tenants and carriers. The landlord's job is never to let the power or cooling fail; the tenants look after their own machines.`,history:`IBM's Robert Sullivan proposed laying out computer rooms in hot and cold aisles in 1992. Digital Realty was founded in 2004 to buy and rent out data-centre buildings. Equinix became a property trust for tax purposes in 2015. Digital Realty bought DuPont Fabros for $7.6 billion in 2017. Digital Realty bought Europe's Interxion for about $8.4 billion in 2020. By 2023 Northern Virginia had more data-centre capacity than anywhere else on Earth.`,facts:[`Digital Realty paid about $8.4 billion for Interxion in 2020.`,`Equinix ran over 240 data centres in 2023.`]},"building:aifactory":{intro:`An AI factory is a data hall built for AI chips: racks so densely packed with graphics processors that each draws as much power as dozens of homes, cooled by liquid flowing through metal plates on the chips. Thousands of chips are wired together by fast networks to work on one model.`,how:`Air cannot carry away that much heat, so coolant is piped to every chip, and the heat is dumped through cooling towers or dry coolers outside. Power arrives in huge feeds, and floors must bear racks weighing well over a tonne. The chips cost far more than the building, so operators work to keep them busy and to open new halls as fast as chips arrive.`,history:`In 2016 Jensen Huang hand-delivered NVIDIA's first DGX-1, a server with eight graphics chips for AI, to OpenAI. By 2023 Huang was calling such data centres AI factories: plants that take in power and data and turn out intelligence. NVIDIA's GB200 NVL72 rack of 2024 linked 72 graphics chips and had to be cooled with liquid. In 2025 Microsoft unveiled Fairwater in Wisconsin, a liquid-cooled AI data centre built for hundreds of thousands of NVIDIA chips.`,facts:[`An average data-centre rack drew about 8 kilowatts in 2020 (Uptime Institute).`,`A GB200 NVL72 rack drew about 120 kilowatts in 2024.`]},"building:hyperscale":{intro:`A hyperscale campus is a group of huge data halls on one site, with its own electrical substation fed by high-voltage lines, built by a cloud giant to run its services and rent computing to the world. Each hall can hold tens of thousands of servers.`,how:`The owner designs everything, from servers to cooling to power, and buys in enormous volume, so its cost per server is lower than anyone else's. Campuses are built in phases as demand grows, on cheap land near strong power lines and fibre routes. The scarcest input is electricity: utilities must build new lines and plants, which takes years.`,history:`Facebook opened its first data centre of its own, at Prineville, Oregon, in 2011, and kept adding halls beside it. Facebook opened a second campus near Altoona, Iowa, in 2014. Synergy Research counted about 500 hyperscale data centres worldwide in 2019. In 2022 Dominion Energy warned that new data centres in Loudoun County, Virginia, might wait years for a power connection. Synergy counted over 1,000 hyperscale data centres in 2024. Amazon, Microsoft, Google and Meta planned more than $300 billion of spending on data centres and equipment in 2025.`,facts:[`Synergy Research counted over 1,000 hyperscale data centres in 2024.`,`The four biggest cloud spenders planned over $300 billion of capital spending in 2025.`]},"building:gigawatt":{intro:`A gigawatt data centre draws as much power as a large nuclear reactor makes, enough for several hundred thousand homes, almost all of it for AI chips training and running models. Such sites hold hundreds of thousands of graphics processors linked as one machine.`,how:`No ordinary grid connection can supply a gigawatt quickly, so builders bring their own: gas turbines on site, giant battery banks to smooth the sudden power swings of training runs, and deals for new transmission lines. Cooling needs vast amounts of water or huge dry coolers. Speed of construction is the race: a site that opens a year earlier trains the next model first.`,history:`In 2024 xAI built its first Colossus cluster in a former Electrolux factory in Memphis, getting 100,000 NVIDIA chips working in 122 days. Local groups protested in 2024 about the gas turbines xAI ran there to power it. OpenAI, Oracle and SoftBank announced their Stargate venture at the White House in January 2025, with a first campus in Abilene, Texas. In 2025 xAI began building Colossus 2, aimed at a gigawatt or more. Amazon's Project Rainier in Indiana came online for Anthropic in 2025 with nearly half a million Trainium chips.`,facts:[`Colossus went from empty factory to 100,000 working GPUs in 122 days in 2024.`,`Stargate's backers promised up to $500 billion of investment in 2025.`]},"building:mega":{intro:`The largest data-centre projects plan several gigawatts on one site: a city of data halls, substations, gas power plants and transmission lines spread over many square kilometres. They are built to train and serve the biggest AI models, and draw as much power as a large city.`,how:`A project this size needs a utility partner to build new power plants and lines, local tax deals, thousands of construction workers and years of work in phases. The chips inside cost more than the buildings, and both may be paid for with debt and outside partners. The supply of turbines, transformers and chips, more than money, sets the pace.`,history:`Meta announced its Hyperion data centre in Richland Parish, Louisiana, in December 2024, at a cost of about $10 billion. In July 2025 Mark Zuckerberg said Hyperion would grow to 5 gigawatts on a site covering much of the area of Manhattan. Louisiana regulators approved three new gas-fired power plants from Entergy to supply it in 2025. In October 2025 Meta arranged about $27 billion of financing for Hyperion with Blue Owl Capital. In 2025 OpenAI said its Stargate sites together were aiming for about 10 gigawatts.`,facts:[`Meta first priced Hyperion at about $10 billion in 2024.`,`Zuckerberg said in 2025 that Hyperion would reach 5 gigawatts.`]},"building:spacedc":{intro:`An orbital compute station is a data centre in space: racks of computer chips on a satellite, powered by wide wings of solar panels that face the Sun almost all the time and cooled by radiators that shed heat into the darkness. It sends its results to Earth by radio or laser.`,how:`In the right orbit sunlight is nearly constant and free, but every kilogram must be launched, so the economics depend on cheap rockets. With no air to carry heat away, it can leave only by radiating from large panels. Chips must survive radiation, and broken parts cannot easily be swapped, so designers rely on spares and software that copes with failures.`,history:`HPE sent its Spaceborne Computer, an ordinary server, to the International Space Station in 2017 to see how commercial hardware coped in orbit. From 2018 to 2020 Microsoft tested the opposite idea, sinking a sealed data centre off Scotland's Orkney Islands. Starcloud launched a satellite carrying an NVIDIA H100 chip in November 2025, the most powerful AI chip yet flown. Google announced Project Suncatcher in November 2025, planning to test its AI chips on solar-powered satellites built with Planet.`,facts:[`Microsoft's sunken servers failed at one eighth the rate of those on land (2020).`,`Starcloud said its 2025 satellite carried a GPU 100 times more powerful than any flown before.`]},"building:gas":{intro:`A gas turbine plant burns natural gas in what is essentially a jet engine bolted to the ground, spinning a generator. Units can be trucked in and running within months, so data centres that cannot wait for the grid use them to make power of their own.`,how:`Air is compressed, mixed with gas and burned, and the hot gas spins the turbine. Simple-cycle units start in minutes but waste much of the heat; combined-cycle plants use the exhaust to raise steam for a second turbine and turn about 60% of the fuel's energy into electricity. Running one means buying gas, holding air permits for the exhaust and dealing with neighbours worried about noise and fumes.`,history:`Brown Boveri built the first gas turbine for a power station, at Neuchâtel in Switzerland, in 1939. From the 1960s jet engines were adapted into quick-starting aeroderivative turbines for power stations. Cheap shale gas made gas the largest source of American electricity in 2016. In 2024 xAI drew protests for running dozens of portable gas turbines at its Memphis data centre. In 2025 makers such as GE Vernova and Siemens Energy were booking turbine orders years ahead as data centres queued for power.`,facts:[`The Neuchâtel turbine of 1939 produced 4 megawatts.`,`Gas made about 40% of America's electricity in 2023.`]},"building:solar":{intro:`A solar farm with batteries spreads rows of panels across open land and pairs them with a battery plant, so that power made by day can be used at night. It needs no fuel, makes no noise and can be built in a year or two.`,how:`Silicon cells turn sunlight straight into electricity; inverters convert it for the grid, and lithium-ion batteries in container-sized units store the surplus. Output follows the sun, so to supply steady power the farm needs several times more panels than its average output, plus storage. Running it is cheap: washing panels, mowing grass and replacing the odd inverter.`,history:`Bell Labs made the first practical silicon solar cell in 1954. The satellite Vanguard 1 ran a radio on solar cells from 1958. ARCO Solar opened the first megawatt-sized solar plant, at Hesperia, California, in 1982. Tesla built the Hornsdale Power Reserve, then the world's biggest battery, in South Australia in 2017. By 2020 the price of solar panels had fallen by about 90% in ten years. Solar was the largest source of new power capacity added worldwide in 2023.`,facts:[`Bell Labs' 1954 cell turned about 6% of sunlight into electricity.`,`Hornsdale's battery could deliver 100 megawatts in 2017.`]},"building:wind":{intro:`A wind farm is a field of tall turbines whose blades turn generators in the wind. Built on hills, plains or out at sea, it makes power with no fuel at all, but only when the wind blows.`,how:`Blades as long as an airliner's wings turn slowly, and a gearbox or a large direct-drive generator turns that into electricity. Output rises steeply with wind speed, so sites are chosen after years of measurements, and taller towers reach steadier winds. Running a farm means crews climbing towers for maintenance, and selling the power under long contracts to utilities or companies.`,history:`Charles Brush built a 12-kilowatt wind-powered dynamo in his garden in Cleveland, Ohio, in 1888. The 1.25-megawatt Smith-Putnam turbine fed the grid in Vermont in 1941. The first wind farm, 20 turbines on Crotched Mountain, New Hampshire, opened in 1980. Denmark built the first offshore wind farm, at Vindeby, in 1991. Google signed its first long-term contract to buy wind power, from Iowa, in 2010. Hornsea 2 off England became the world's largest offshore wind farm in 2022, at 1.3 gigawatts.`,facts:[`The Smith-Putnam turbine of 1941 had blades 53 metres across.`,`Hornsea 2 had 165 turbines in 2022.`]},"building:geo":{intro:`A geothermal plant taps the heat of the Earth: wells bring up steam or hot water from deep underground to spin turbines, and the cooled water is pumped back down. It runs day and night, whatever the weather.`,how:`The best sites are where hot rock lies near the surface, around volcanoes and fault lines. Drilling is the big cost and the big risk: a dry or cool well wastes millions. Newer enhanced systems borrow oil-field methods, drilling sideways and cracking hot dry rock so water can circulate through it, which could open sites far from any volcano.`,history:`Prince Piero Ginori Conti lit light bulbs with geothermal steam at Larderello, Italy, in 1904. Larderello's first commercial geothermal power station opened in 1913. The Geysers in California began producing power in 1960 and grew into the world's largest geothermal field. Iceland's Hellisheiði plant opened in 2006. Fervo Energy's pilot with Google in Nevada began sending power to the grid in 2023, using sideways drilling from the oil industry. Meta signed a deal with Sage Geosystems for geothermal power in 2024.`,facts:[`Geothermal plants made about 30% of Iceland's electricity in the 2010s.`,`Larderello's first power station of 1913 produced 250 kilowatts.`]},"building:nuclear":{intro:`A nuclear restart brings a closed reactor back into service: engineers inspect and repair it, the regulator approves it again, and a buyer signs up for all its power for decades. Reactors run day and night with no carbon emissions, which is exactly what a data centre wants.`,how:`A reactor heats water with the energy of splitting uranium atoms, making steam to drive turbines, and is refuelled only every year or two. Restarting one that closed for economic reasons is faster and cheaper than building new, but it needs new fuel, rehired staff and a long safety review. The deal works when a buyer guarantees a good price for every megawatt-hour for twenty years or so.`,history:`Three Mile Island's Unit 2 in Pennsylvania partly melted down in 1979, beside its twin, Unit 1. Unit 1, restarted after the accident, closed in 2019 because it could not compete with cheap gas. In March 2024 Amazon bought a data-centre campus beside Pennsylvania's Susquehanna nuclear plant for $650 million. In September 2024 Microsoft agreed to buy all the power of a restarted Unit 1, renamed the Crane Clean Energy Center, for 20 years. In 2025 Michigan's Palisades plant, closed in 2022, became the first in America cleared to restart. In October 2025 Google and NextEra agreed to restart Iowa's Duane Arnold plant.`,facts:[`Constellation planned to spend about $1.6 billion restarting Three Mile Island Unit 1 (2024).`,`Amazon paid $650 million for the Susquehanna campus in 2024.`]},"building:smr":{intro:`Small modular reactors are nuclear reactors a fraction of the usual size, designed to be built in factories, shipped in pieces and put together on site. A few can be grouped beside a factory or data centre to give steady power for decades.`,how:`Smaller cores can be cooled by natural circulation and passive safety features rather than pumps that need power, which simplifies the plant. Building many identical units should bring costs down as factories learn, but the first ones are expensive and every design must pass a long safety review. Some use new fuels and coolants, such as molten salt or helium gas.`,history:`Small reactors first powered ships: the submarine USS Nautilus sailed on nuclear power in 1955. Russia's floating nuclear plant Akademik Lomonosov, with two small reactors, began commercial operation in 2020. In 2023 NuScale became the first small modular design certified by the US regulator, but its first project, in Utah, was cancelled that November as costs rose. China's HTR-PM, cooled by helium, entered commercial operation in December 2023. In October 2024 Google ordered reactors from Kairos Power and Amazon invested in X-energy. In 2025 Ontario began building a GE Hitachi BWRX-300 at Darlington.`,facts:[`Akademik Lomonosov's two reactors produced about 70 megawatts in 2020.`,`Google's 2024 deal with Kairos covered up to 500 megawatts of reactors.`]},"building:fusion":{intro:`A fusion plant would make power the way the Sun does, by fusing light hydrogen atoms into helium at over a hundred million degrees. Its fuel can be drawn from seawater and lithium, and it leaves no long-lived waste, which is why people have chased it for decades.`,how:`The hot fuel, a plasma, must be held hot and dense for long enough that fusion gives out more energy than it takes to run. Tokamaks hold it in a doughnut of powerful magnetic fields; laser machines crush tiny pellets of fuel instead. A working plant must also capture the heat, breed new fuel from lithium and survive a storm of fast neutrons.`,history:`In 1968 Soviet scientists reported temperatures in their T-3 tokamak that astonished Western physicists. Europe's JET made 16 megawatts of fusion power for a moment in 1997. Construction of ITER, a giant tokamak backed by 35 nations, began in southern France in 2010. On 5 December 2022 America's National Ignition Facility got more energy out of a fuel pellet than its lasers put in. In 2023 Microsoft agreed to buy power from Helion Energy's first fusion plant, still to be built. In 2025 Google agreed to buy 200 megawatts from Commonwealth Fusion Systems' first plant, planned for Virginia.`,facts:[`JET produced 59 megajoules of fusion energy in five seconds in 2021.`,`NIF's 2022 shot gave 3.15 megajoules from 2.05 megajoules of laser light.`]},"building:studio":{intro:`A chip design studio is where engineers turn an idea for a chip into a layout a factory can make. They describe the circuit in code, simulate it, check it against the factory's rules and send the final pattern off to be made, a moment called tape-out.`,how:`Designers write the chip in hardware description languages, and design software, licensed by the seat for large yearly fees, turns that into billions of placed and wired transistors. Before anything is made the chip is tested in simulation and on emulators, big machines that mimic it in hardware. A mistake found after tape-out costs months and millions, so checking takes most of the time and staff.`,history:`Carver Mead and Lynn Conway's 1980 book Introduction to VLSI Systems showed how to design chips without owning a factory. Xilinx, founded in 1984, was among the first fabless chip companies, designing chips that others made. Synopsys was founded in 1986 to sell logic synthesis, software that turns code into circuits. Cadence formed in 1988 from the merger of SDA Systems and ECAD. Apple bought the chip designer PA Semi in 2008, and its first chip of its own design, the A4, appeared in 2010. Google revealed in 2016 that its engineers had designed their own AI chip, the TPU.`,facts:[`Apple paid $278 million for PA Semi in 2008.`,`Designing a 5 nm chip cost about $540 million in 2020 (IBS).`]},"building:minifab":{intro:`A fab is a chip factory: a building around a cleanroom where silicon wafers are printed, etched, coated and baked hundreds of times to build up layers of transistors and wiring. The air is filtered until almost no dust remains, and workers wear full-body suits.`,how:`Each wafer passes through hundreds of steps on machines costing millions each, carried between tools by overhead robots. Yield, the share of chips that work, decides profit, so engineers chase every source of defects. A fab runs day and night, every day of the year, because its machines are too expensive to stand idle, and it must be re-equipped for every new process.`,history:`In 1960 Willis Whitfield at Sandia Laboratories invented the laminar-flow cleanroom, sweeping dust away with a steady stream of filtered air. Intel's first fab, in Mountain View, began making memory chips in 1969. TSMC began in 1987 with a single fab in Hsinchu. A leading-edge fab cost over $20 billion by 2020. TSMC began building its first fab in Arizona in 2021. Intel announced chip factories in Ohio worth $20 billion in 2022.`,facts:[`TSMC's plans for Arizona reached $165 billion in 2025.`,`Intel's Ohio plans of 2022 started at $20 billion.`]},"building:gigafab":{intro:`A GigaFab is TSMC's name for its giant wafer factories, the biggest chip plants in the world: several cleanrooms on one site, each as long as a few football pitches, linked by overhead rails that carry wafers between thousands of tools.`,how:`Size brings savings: engineers, spare parts, chemicals and lessons learned are shared across the site, and a new process can be rolled out to several cleanrooms at once. Such a site needs its own water treatment, gas plants and power supply on the scale of a town. Only a company with steady orders from many customers can keep one full.`,history:`TSMC's Fab 12 in Hsinchu began making chips on 300 mm wafers around 2001. Samsung's Pyeongtaek Line 1, among the largest chip plants in the world, began production in 2017. TSMC's Fab 18 in Tainan began volume production of 5 nm chips in 2020. TSMC's Fab 18 began making 3 nm chips in December 2022. In 2025 TSMC began making 2 nm chips at new GigaFabs in Hsinchu and Kaohsiung.`,facts:[`TSMC ran four GigaFabs in Taiwan in 2020: Fabs 12, 14, 15 and 18.`,`TSMC's Fab 18 began 3 nm production in 2022.`]},"building:memfab":{intro:`A memory fab makes DRAM chips by the billion, and its stacking line turns some of them into HBM: dies are thinned, drilled with thousands of tiny vertical connections called through-silicon vias, and piled up to a dozen high on a base chip. The finished stacks sit beside AI processors.`,how:`DRAM fabs make one kind of chip in vast volume, so cost per bit and yield decide everything. Stacking adds new risks: one bad die spoils a whole stack, so testing and bonding skill set profits. Memory prices rise and fall with the market, so makers must decide years ahead how much to build, and whoever gets HBM right wins the AI chipmakers' orders.`,history:`Samsung began making DRAM in 1983. JEDEC published the first HBM standard in 2013, after work by AMD and SK hynix. SK hynix opened its M16 fab in Icheon in 2021. Micron broke ground on a new memory fab in Boise, Idaho, in 2022. SK hynix's HBM3 went into NVIDIA's H100 in 2022. In 2024 SK hynix and Micron said their HBM for the year ahead was already sold out.`,facts:[`HBM3E stacks held 8 or 12 DRAM dies in 2024.`,`Micron planned about $15 billion for its Boise fab in 2022.`]},"building:waferplant":{intro:`A silicon wafer plant grows the pure crystals every chip starts from. Ultra-pure silicon is melted in a quartz crucible, a seed crystal is slowly drawn upward to grow a single-crystal ingot weighing hundreds of kilograms, and saws and polishers turn it into mirror-flat wafers.`,how:`Crystal pullers run for days per ingot in sealed furnaces filled with argon, turning the crystal and the crucible to keep the growth perfect. The ingots are ground round, sliced with wire saws, lapped, etched and polished, then cleaned and inspected for invisible flaws. The process needs plenty of power and very pure water, and each chipmaker must test and approve a plant's wafers before buying.`,history:`Gordon Teal at Texas Instruments grew the silicon crystals for the first silicon transistors in 1954. Monsanto became a leading American wafer maker and sold its wafer business, MEMC, in 1989. SUMCO was formed in 1999 from the silicon arms of Sumitomo Metal and Mitsubishi Materials. The first 300 mm wafers went into volume production around 2001. GlobalWafers began building a 300 mm wafer plant in Sherman, Texas, in 2022.`,facts:[`A 300 mm wafer has 2.25 times the area of a 200 mm one (2001).`,`Wafer makers sold a record $13.8 billion of wafers in 2022 (SEMI).`]},"building:packaging":{intro:`An advanced packaging plant joins several chips into one product. Processor dies and stacks of memory are placed side by side on a thin slice of silicon called an interposer, wired together with connections far finer than any circuit board can carry, and sealed as a single package.`,how:`Packaging was once the cheap last step: cutting up wafers, gluing chips into plastic and wiring their pins. Big AI chips need packaging almost as precise as chipmaking itself, done in cleanrooms with lithography, microscopic solder bumps and careful bonding. When demand jumps, packaging capacity rather than chip output can be the bottleneck.`,history:`IBM introduced flip-chip bonding, mounting chips upside down on tiny solder bumps, in 1964. TSMC's CoWoS packaging first joined four chips on one silicon interposer for Xilinx's Virtex-7 2000T in 2011, and TSMC offered it to all its customers from 2012. TSMC's InFO packaging won Apple's A10 chip for the iPhone 7 in 2016. AMD's EPYC processors of 2017 joined several smaller chips, called chiplets, in one package. Shortages of CoWoS capacity held back NVIDIA's H100 supply in 2023. TSMC roughly doubled its CoWoS capacity in 2024.`,facts:[`Xilinx's Virtex-7 2000T of 2011 held 6.8 billion transistors across four chips.`,`TSMC's InFO won Apple's iPhone processors in 2016.`]},"building:litho":{intro:`The factory where extreme ultraviolet lithography machines are built: cleanrooms in Veldhoven in the Netherlands where engineers assemble the most complex machines sold anywhere, from parts made by hundreds of suppliers across Europe and beyond.`,how:`Each machine fires a laser at falling droplets of molten tin tens of thousands of times a second, making a plasma that glows at a wavelength of 13.5 nm. Mirrors polished almost to the atom steer the light, all inside a vacuum. Assembly and testing take months, then the machine is taken apart, flown to the customer in pieces and rebuilt there. Key parts come from a few suppliers, such as Zeiss for the mirrors and Trumpf for the lasers.`,history:`In 1997 Intel, AMD and Motorola formed the EUV LLC with three US national laboratories to develop extreme ultraviolet lithography. ASML bought Cymer, the San Diego maker of its light sources, in 2013. ASML shipped its first production EUV scanner, the NXE:3400B, in 2017. EUV machines began printing chips in volume at TSMC and Samsung in 2019. ASML shipped its first High-NA EUV machine, about the size of a double-decker bus, to Intel in Oregon in December 2023.`,facts:[`An EUV machine hit about 50,000 tin droplets a second in 2017.`,`ASML paid about $2.5 billion for Cymer in 2013.`]},"building:qlab":{intro:`A quantum lab houses quantum computers and the machinery that keeps them working: dilution refrigerators, tall gold-plated cylinders hung from a frame, that cool superconducting chips to about a hundredth of a degree above absolute zero. Racks of microwave electronics send the pulses that control the qubits.`,how:`The refrigerator cools in stages, the last using a mix of two kinds of helium, and takes a day or more to reach its working temperature. Every wire into the cold stage leaks heat and noise, so wiring becomes a big engineering problem as qubit counts grow. Physicists recalibrate the qubits daily, since their behaviour drifts, and run experiments for remote users around the clock.`,history:`Heinz London proposed the dilution refrigerator in 1951. The first working one was built in the Netherlands in 1964. Yasunobu Nakamura's team at NEC made the first superconducting qubit in 1999. Finland's Bluefors, founded in 2008, became the leading maker of dilution refrigerators for quantum labs. Google ran its Sycamore experiment in a lab in Santa Barbara in 2019. IBM showed Goldeneye, a dilution refrigerator big enough for a future machine with a million qubits, in 2020.`,facts:[`Nakamura's 1999 qubit held its state for only about a nanosecond.`,`Superconducting qubits ran at about 10 to 20 thousandths of a degree above absolute zero in 2019.`]},"building:depot":{intro:`A robotaxi depot is the base for a city's fleet of driverless cars: a big car park or garage with rows of fast chargers, bays where cars are cleaned inside and out, and workshops where technicians check and repair their sensors. Cars return here between rides to charge and wait for demand.`,how:`Cameras and lidar must be spotless, so cars pass through bays where sensors are wiped and recalibrated. Staff vacuum seats, collect lost property and swap tyres, while a control room watches the fleet and remote helpers advise cars that get stuck. Location matters: a depot near busy areas cuts the empty miles to the first pickup, and neighbours must live with the traffic and noise.`,history:`In 2018 Waymo launched its robotaxi service in Phoenix, run from depots that cleaned and serviced its Chrysler Pacifica minivans. In 2019 Waymo chose a factory in Detroit to fit its sensors to cars. In August 2024 neighbours of a Waymo car park in San Francisco complained that its cars woke them by honking at each other at night. In 2025 Waymo and Magna opened a factory in Mesa, Arizona, to fit out its electric Jaguars. In June 2025 protesters in Los Angeles set several Waymo cars on fire. Zoox opened free robotaxi rides to the public in Las Vegas in 2025.`,facts:[`Waymo had about 1,500 robotaxis on the road in 2025.`,`Waymo's Mesa factory of 2025 was designed to build tens of thousands of cars a year.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_MARKETS};